		{"key": "about-wiki", "title": "About this wiki", "category": "wiki", "html": "<h1>About this wiki</h1><p>The wiki is available in two places with the same articles:</p><ul><li><strong>in the game</strong>: the <strong>Wiki</strong> button on the main menu and on the game files screen, the <strong>Wiki</strong> button under the Etherlords II Esc menus in battle and on the map, or <strong>Shift+F1</strong> on any screen. The game waits while you read. <strong>Esc</strong>, <strong>F1</strong> or <strong>Close</strong> returns to it.</li><li><strong>on the web</strong> at <a href=\"https://demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki/\">demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki</a>, free to read without game files, an account or the engine. The browser version's start page links to it.</li></ul><h2>Languages</h2><p>Every article is written in English and Russian. Switch with <strong>EN / RU</strong> at the top of the in-game wiki or with the language list on the website. Your choice is remembered. Until you choose, the in-game wiki follows the language of your game's texts and the website follows your browser.</p><p>Names of game things (races, phases, menus) follow the original games' own English and Russian texts where they apply.</p><h2>What it covers</h2><p>The guides explain how to play the games on this engine and how the engine differs from the originals. The <strong>encyclopedia</strong> has a page for every creature, sorcery, enchantment, artifact, skill and specialization of both games, with its statistics, prices and description. On the website, creature pages show a picture of the creature; in the game they show the creature itself, which you can turn and zoom.</p><p>The encyclopedia in the game is built from your own copy, so it follows your game's language and data. The website's encyclopedia was exported from the original Russian and English (GOG) editions. Names, descriptions and pictures of the games' content belong to their owners.</p><p>To preview attacks, hit reactions, deaths, and other actions with their original sounds, press <strong>Shift+F1</strong> in the game and open a creature page. Choose an action below the 3D model and press <strong>Play</strong>. See <a href=\"wiki://creatures\">About creatures</a> for the full controls.</p>", "search": "about this wiki the wiki is available in two places with the same articles: in the game: the wiki button on the main menu and on the game files screen, the wiki button under the etherlords ii esc menus in battle and on the map, or shift+f1 on any screen. the game waits while you read. esc, f1 or close returns to it. on the web at demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki, free to read without game files, an account or the engine. the browser version's start page links to it. languages every article is written in english and russian. switch with en / ru at the top of the in-game wiki or with the language list on the website. your choice is remembered. until you choose, the in-game wiki follows the language of your game's texts and the website follows your browser. names of game things (races, phases, menus) follow the original games' own english and russian texts where they apply. what it covers the guides explain how to play the games on this engine and how the engine differs from the originals. the encyclopedia has a page for every creature, sorcery, enchantment, artifact, skill and specialization of both games, with its statistics, prices and description. on the website, creature pages show a picture of the creature; in the game they show the creature itself, which you can turn and zoom. the encyclopedia in the game is built from your own copy, so it follows your game's language and data. the website's encyclopedia was exported from the original russian and english (gog) editions. names, descriptions and pictures of the games' content belong to their owners. to preview attacks, hit reactions, deaths, and other actions with their original sounds, press shift+f1 in the game and open a creature page. choose an action below the 3d model and press play. see about creatures for the full controls."},
		{"key": "creatures", "title": "About creatures", "category": "all-creatures", "html": "<h1>About creatures</h1><p>Creatures are summoned with creature <a href=\"wiki://spells\">spells</a> and fight for their hero.</p><h2>Power and toughness</h2><p>Each creature has <strong>power</strong>, the damage it deals in combat, and <strong>toughness</strong>, the damage it can take. Damage on a creature heals at the end of each turn, so a creature is destroyed only when it takes as much damage as its toughness within one turn.</p><h2>Resting</h2><p>A creature that attacks or uses certain abilities <strong>rests</strong> and stays down until its owner's next turn. A newly summoned creature also rests unless it has <strong>Haste</strong>. Resting creatures cannot block.</p><h2>Abilities</h2><p>Most creatures have abilities, shown as icons. See <a href=\"wiki://abilities\">Creature abilities</a> for the common ones and <a href=\"wiki://attack-defence\">Attack and defence</a> for how they affect combat.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>A hero can control at most 10 creatures. Destroyed creatures go to their owner's graveyard; some spells bring them back.</p><p>All creatures of both games: <a href=\"wiki://all-creatures\">the list of creatures</a>.</p><h2>Preview animations and sounds</h2><p>Press <strong>F1</strong> in the game and open a page from the <a href=\"wiki://all-creatures\">creature list</a>. Use the menu below the 3D model to choose an animation, then press <strong>Play</strong>. Depending on the creature, you can preview attacks, ranged attacks, hit reactions, deaths, and other actions. To return to the idle pose, select <strong>Idle</strong> and press <strong>Play</strong> again.</p><p>Drag the model to turn it and scroll to zoom. When the page offers models for both games, choose <strong>Etherlords I</strong> or <strong>Etherlords II</strong> to see the animations available in that edition.</p><p>The viewer plays the original creature sounds when a recording is available. It uses the <strong>Effects</strong> volume from <a href=\"wiki://options\">Options</a>. The web wiki shows still images of creatures.</p>", "search": "about creatures creatures are summoned with creature spells and fight for their hero. power and toughness each creature has power, the damage it deals in combat, and toughness, the damage it can take. damage on a creature heals at the end of each turn, so a creature is destroyed only when it takes as much damage as its toughness within one turn. resting a creature that attacks or uses certain abilities rests and stays down until its owner's next turn. a newly summoned creature also rests unless it has haste. resting creatures cannot block. abilities most creatures have abilities, shown as icons. see creature abilities for the common ones and attack and defence for how they affect combat. limits a hero can control at most 10 creatures. destroyed creatures go to their owner's graveyard; some spells bring them back. all creatures of both games: the list of creatures. preview animations and sounds press f1 in the game and open a page from the creature list. use the menu below the 3d model to choose an animation, then press play. depending on the creature, you can preview attacks, ranged attacks, hit reactions, deaths, and other actions. to return to the idle pose, select idle and press play again. drag the model to turn it and scroll to zoom. when the page offers models for both games, choose etherlords i or etherlords ii to see the animations available in that edition. the viewer plays the original creature sounds when a recording is available. it uses the effects volume from options. the web wiki shows still images of creatures."},
		{"key": "spells", "title": "About spells", "category": "all-spells", "html": "<h1>About spells</h1><p>Everything a hero does in battle is a spell. There are three kinds:</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Creature</td><td>Summons a creature that stays until it is destroyed. See <a href=\"wiki://creatures\">Creatures</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Enchantment</td><td>Stays in play and changes a creature, a hero or the whole battlefield.</td></tr><tr><td>Sorcery</td><td>Acts once and is gone: damage, healing, destruction, extra spells and more.</td></tr></table><h2>Cost and casting</h2><p>Each spell costs ether, shown on its card. Some spells have a variable cost and become stronger with the extra ether you spend. A few cost health instead. You can cast spells in your <a href=\"wiki://combat-phases\">Attack/Cast Phase</a> as long as you can pay for them and they have a target; in the Block Phase only a few spells are allowed.</p><h2>Rarity</h2><p>Spells are cantrips, common, rare or unique. <strong>Cantrips</strong> are simple, unlimited spells; in Etherlords a spell whose runes are used up is replaced by a cantrip of the same colour. Rarer spells are more powerful and more expensive in shops and in the duel editor.</p><h2>Artifacts</h2><p>Some spells are artifacts: battle items with a limited number of uses. See <a href=\"wiki://runes-artifacts\">Runes and artifacts</a>.</p><p>The games' own spellbook and Encyclopedia (Etherlords II) screens describe every spell.</p><p>All sorceries and enchantments of both games: <a href=\"wiki://all-spells\">the list of spells</a>.</p>", "search": "about spells everything a hero does in battle is a spell. there are three kinds: creature summons a creature that stays until it is destroyed. see creatures. enchantment stays in play and changes a creature, a hero or the whole battlefield. sorcery acts once and is gone: damage, healing, destruction, extra spells and more. cost and casting each spell costs ether, shown on its card. some spells have a variable cost and become stronger with the extra ether you spend. a few cost health instead. you can cast spells in your attack/cast phase as long as you can pay for them and they have a target; in the block phase only a few spells are allowed. rarity spells are cantrips, common, rare or unique. cantrips are simple, unlimited spells; in etherlords a spell whose runes are used up is replaced by a cantrip of the same colour. rarer spells are more powerful and more expensive in shops and in the duel editor. artifacts some spells are artifacts: battle items with a limited number of uses. see runes and artifacts. the games' own spellbook and encyclopedia (etherlords ii) screens describe every spell. all sorceries and enchantments of both games: the list of spells."},
		{"key": "runes-artifacts", "title": "Runes and artifacts", "category": "all-artifacts", "html": "<h1>Runes and artifacts</h1><h2>Runes (Etherlords)</h2><p>In Etherlords every spell in the book has <strong>runes</strong>: charges that limit how often it can be cast. When the runes of a spell are used up, a drawn copy is replaced by a random cantrip of the same colour. Buy runes in Rune Shops and Sorcerer's Labs on the <a href=\"wiki://adventure-map\">map</a>; the <strong>Resources</strong> skill raises how many a spell can hold. Cantrips need no runes.</p><h2>Spell charges (Etherlords II)</h2><p>In Etherlords II every spell in the bag starts each battle with 1, 2, 4 or 7 charges, depending on the hero's <strong>Resources</strong> skill. The bag holds 16 spells, at most 4 copies of one spell.</p><h2>Artifacts</h2><p>Both games have <strong>artifacts</strong>: battle items with charges. An artifact can be used from a given round of the battle and then needs a number of rounds before it can be used again. A hero can use artifacts once per battle in total, or 2, 4 or 8 times with the <strong>Artificer</strong> skill.</p><ul><li>In Etherlords, artifacts are found on the map.</li><li>In Etherlords II, heroes keep their artifacts and their charges after battle. Blank artifacts can be made into new ones and spent artifacts can be recharged.</li></ul><p>See <a href=\"wiki://heroes\">Heroes and levels</a> for the skills mentioned here.</p><p>All artifacts of both games: <a href=\"wiki://all-artifacts\">the list of artifacts</a>.</p>", "search": "runes and artifacts runes (etherlords) in etherlords every spell in the book has runes: charges that limit how often it can be cast. when the runes of a spell are used up, a drawn copy is replaced by a random cantrip of the same colour. buy runes in rune shops and sorcerer's labs on the map; the resources skill raises how many a spell can hold. cantrips need no runes. spell charges (etherlords ii) in etherlords ii every spell in the bag starts each battle with 1, 2, 4 or 7 charges, depending on the hero's resources skill. the bag holds 16 spells, at most 4 copies of one spell. artifacts both games have artifacts: battle items with charges. an artifact can be used from a given round of the battle and then needs a number of rounds before it can be used again. a hero can use artifacts once per battle in total, or 2, 4 or 8 times with the artificer skill. in etherlords, artifacts are found on the map. in etherlords ii, heroes keep their artifacts and their charges after battle. blank artifacts can be made into new ones and spent artifacts can be recharged. see heroes and levels for the skills mentioned here. all artifacts of both games: the list of artifacts."},
		{"key": "heroes", "title": "Heroes and levels", "category": "all-skills", "html": "<h1>Heroes and levels</h1><p>Heroes gain experience from battles and grow through 20 levels. Each level adds health and ether income in battle.</p><h2>Skills</h2><p>Each new level gives a skill point and an offer of three random skills (in Etherlords II, <strong>Resources</strong> is always offered first while the hero does not have it). A hero knows at most five skills, each up to rank 3.</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Strength</td><td>+10, 20 or 30% health.</td></tr><tr><td>Regeneration</td><td>Restores health on some turns.</td></tr><tr><td>Concentration</td><td>An extra spell every few turns.</td></tr><tr><td>Channeling</td><td>Delays your ether disturbance and hastens the opponent's.</td></tr><tr><td>Learning</td><td>More experience.</td></tr><tr><td>Luck</td><td>Better chances for the hero's specialisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Suppression</td><td>Lowers the opponent's luck.</td></tr><tr><td>Artificer</td><td>More artifact uses per battle.</td></tr><tr><td>Resources</td><td>More runes per spell (Etherlords) or more charges per spell (Etherlords II).</td></tr><tr><td>Bargain</td><td>Lower shop prices (Etherlords).</td></tr></table><p>Etherlords also has skills for the map, such as <strong>Mobility</strong>, <strong>Estates</strong> and <strong>Looting</strong>. Its <strong>Ethereal Attack</strong> and <strong>Ethereal Defense</strong> have no effect, exactly as in the original game.</p><h2>Specialisation</h2><p>Every hero has one <strong>specialisation</strong>: a chance-based talent in battle, such as a creature dealing double damage. Its chance grows with the battle's level. <strong>Luck</strong> and the opponent's <strong>Suppression</strong> change it.</p><p>See <a href=\"wiki://runes-artifacts\">Runes and artifacts</a> and <a href=\"wiki://combat\">How a battle works</a>.</p><p>All <a href=\"wiki://all-skills\">hero skills</a> and <a href=\"wiki://all-specializations\">specializations</a> of both games.</p>", "search": "heroes and levels heroes gain experience from battles and grow through 20 levels. each level adds health and ether income in battle. skills each new level gives a skill point and an offer of three random skills (in etherlords ii, resources is always offered first while the hero does not have it). a hero knows at most five skills, each up to rank 3. strength +10, 20 or 30% health. regeneration restores health on some turns. concentration an extra spell every few turns. channeling delays your ether disturbance and hastens the opponent's. learning more experience. luck better chances for the hero's specialisation. suppression lowers the opponent's luck. artificer more artifact uses per battle. resources more runes per spell (etherlords) or more charges per spell (etherlords ii). bargain lower shop prices (etherlords). etherlords also has skills for the map, such as mobility, estates and looting. its ethereal attack and ethereal defense have no effect, exactly as in the original game. specialisation every hero has one specialisation: a chance-based talent in battle, such as a creature dealing double damage. its chance grows with the battle's level. luck and the opponent's suppression change it. see runes and artifacts and how a battle works. all hero skills and specializations of both games."},
		{"key": "races", "title": "The four races", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>The four races</h1><p>Etherlords has four races, each drawing on its own colour of ether. A race's spells share a style of play.</p><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Race</th><th>Ether</th><th>Style</th></tr><tr><td>Chaots</td><td>red Ether of Flame</td><td>direct damage and aggressive creatures</td></tr><tr><td>Vitals</td><td>green Ether of Nature</td><td>strong creatures, growth and healing</td></tr><tr><td>Kinets</td><td>blue Ether of Air</td><td>flyers, tricks and control over the opponent's spells</td></tr><tr><td>Synthets</td><td>black Ether of the Machine</td><td>destruction, weakening and draining the opponent</td></tr></table><p>Etherlords II adds <strong>pale</strong> (colourless) spells and Diamanda, the ancient creation of heroes' magic, the subject of its last <a href=\"wiki://campaign-el2\">campaign</a>. Pale spells fit any spellbook.</p><h2>Races and spellbooks</h2><p>In a <a href=\"wiki://duel\">duel</a>, a hero's spellbook must match the hero's race (pale spells are always allowed in Etherlords II). In the campaigns a hero can collect spells of any colour.</p><p>See <a href=\"wiki://spells\">Spells</a> for the kinds of spells each race uses.</p>", "search": "the four races etherlords has four races, each drawing on its own colour of ether. a race's spells share a style of play. chaots red ether of flame direct damage and aggressive creatures vitals green ether of nature strong creatures, growth and healing kinets blue ether of air flyers, tricks and control over the opponent's spells synthets black ether of the machine destruction, weakening and draining the opponent etherlords ii adds pale (colourless) spells and diamanda, the ancient creation of heroes' magic, the subject of its last campaign. pale spells fit any spellbook. races and spellbooks in a duel, a hero's spellbook must match the hero's race (pale spells are always allowed in etherlords ii). in the campaigns a hero can collect spells of any colour. see spells for the kinds of spells each race uses."},
		{"key": "combat", "title": "How a battle works", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>How a battle works</h1><p>Every battle in Etherlords is a duel between two heroes, the Etherlords. Each hero casts spells from a spellbook: creatures fight, enchantments change the battle and sorceries act at once. The first hero whose health falls to 0 loses. Demiurges follows the original rules of each game; the same structure applies to both games, with differences in the numbers.</p><h2>Health</h2><p>A hero's health grows with the hero's level. The <strong>Strength</strong> skill adds 10, 20 or 30%. In campaign battles the hero starts with the health they have on the map. If both heroes reach 0 at the same moment, the battle is a draw.</p><h2>Ether and ether channels</h2><p>Spells cost <strong>ether</strong>. Each hero has <strong>ether channels</strong>, which start at 0 and grow by 0 to 2 at the start of every turn, faster for higher-level heroes. Each turn the hero's ether is refilled to the number of channels, so ether you do not spend is lost. Some Etherlords II spells also need a minimum number of channels, and spells with a variable cost take as much extra ether as you choose.</p><p><strong>Ether disturbance</strong> stops a battle from dragging on: after a number of turns that grows with the hero's level, the hero starts losing health every turn. In Etherlords the damage grows by 1 each turn; in Etherlords II it grows much faster. The <strong>Channeling</strong> skill delays your own disturbance and hastens your opponent's.</p><h2>The hand and the spellbook</h2><p>Both heroes start with <strong>5 spells</strong> in hand, dealt in turn. After that, every turn starts with one draw (the side that begins does not draw on the very first turn). Extra draws come from the <strong>Concentration</strong> skill and some spells. At the end of your turn you keep at most 5 spells.</p><p>Spells are drawn at random from the spellbook, without repeats until every spell in the book has been drawn. How often a spell can be used differs between the games: see <a href=\"wiki://runes-artifacts\">Runes and artifacts</a>.</p><h2>Creatures</h2><p>A hero controls at most <strong>10 creatures</strong>. A newly summoned creature rests: it cannot attack or use abilities that need it to rest until its owner's next turn, unless it has <strong>Haste</strong>. Damage on creatures heals at the end of every turn; destroyed creatures go to their owner's graveyard. See <a href=\"wiki://creatures\">Creatures</a> and <a href=\"wiki://abilities\">Creature abilities</a>.</p><h2>Next</h2><ul><li><a href=\"wiki://combat-phases\">Turn and phases</a>: what happens in which order.</li><li><a href=\"wiki://attack-defence\">Attack and defence</a>: how attackers, blockers and damage work.</li><li><a href=\"wiki://spells\">Spells</a>: the three kinds of spell.</li></ul>", "search": "how a battle works every battle in etherlords is a duel between two heroes, the etherlords. each hero casts spells from a spellbook: creatures fight, enchantments change the battle and sorceries act at once. the first hero whose health falls to 0 loses. demiurges follows the original rules of each game; the same structure applies to both games, with differences in the numbers. health a hero's health grows with the hero's level. the strength skill adds 10, 20 or 30%. in campaign battles the hero starts with the health they have on the map. if both heroes reach 0 at the same moment, the battle is a draw. ether and ether channels spells cost ether. each hero has ether channels, which start at 0 and grow by 0 to 2 at the start of every turn, faster for higher-level heroes. each turn the hero's ether is refilled to the number of channels, so ether you do not spend is lost. some etherlords ii spells also need a minimum number of channels, and spells with a variable cost take as much extra ether as you choose. ether disturbance stops a battle from dragging on: after a number of turns that grows with the hero's level, the hero starts losing health every turn. in etherlords the damage grows by 1 each turn; in etherlords ii it grows much faster. the channeling skill delays your own disturbance and hastens your opponent's. the hand and the spellbook both heroes start with 5 spells in hand, dealt in turn. after that, every turn starts with one draw (the side that begins does not draw on the very first turn). extra draws come from the concentration skill and some spells. at the end of your turn you keep at most 5 spells. spells are drawn at random from the spellbook, without repeats until every spell in the book has been drawn. how often a spell can be used differs between the games: see runes and artifacts. creatures a hero controls at most 10 creatures. a newly summoned creature rests: it cannot attack or use abilities that need it to rest until its owner's next turn, unless it has haste. damage on creatures heals at the end of every turn; destroyed creatures go to their owner's graveyard. see creatures and creature abilities. next turn and phases: what happens in which order. attack and defence: how attackers, blockers and damage work. spells: the three kinds of spell."},
		{"key": "combat-phases", "title": "Turn and phases", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>Turn and phases</h1><p>A turn belongs to one hero, the attacker. It has two phases: the attacker's <strong>Attack/Cast Phase</strong>, then the opponent's <strong>Block Phase</strong>. Then the heroes swap roles and the next turn begins. Use the <strong>End of Phase</strong> button, <strong>Ctrl+E</strong> in Etherlords, or <strong>Tab</strong> in Etherlords II to finish your phase. <strong>Space</strong> skips the current presentation.</p><h2>Start of the turn</h2><p>At the start of the attacker's turn, in this order:</p><ol><li><a href=\"wiki://combat\">Ether disturbance</a> damage, once it has started.</li><li>Healing from the <strong>Regeneration</strong> skill.</li><li>The attacker's creatures recover (stand up) and stop being newly summoned, except under spells that keep them down.</li><li>The attacker draws a spell.</li><li>Ether channels grow and ether is refilled to the number of channels.</li><li>Enchantments that act at the start of the turn take effect.</li></ol><h2>Attack/Cast Phase</h2><p>The attacker casts spells and chooses which ready creatures attack. Creatures that rest, were just summoned (without <strong>Haste</strong>) or cannot attack stay back. <strong>Berserk</strong> creatures must attack. When the attacker ends the phase, the chosen creatures attack. If no creature attacks, the Block Phase is skipped.</p><h2>Block Phase</h2><p>Now the defender acts: they assign blockers (and may cast the few spells allowed in this phase) to the attacking creatures. Each ready creature can block one attacker, and several creatures can block the same attacker. When the defender ends the phase, combat damage is dealt: see <a href=\"wiki://attack-defence\">Attack and defence</a>.</p><h2>End of the turn</h2><ol><li>The attacker discards down to 5 spells in hand.</li><li>Damage on all creatures heals.</li><li>Enchantments that act at the end of the turn take effect.</li></ol><h2>Who begins</h2><p>In a <a href=\"wiki://duel\">duel</a>, a coin decides who attacks first in every round. In campaign battles the map decides, as in the original games.</p><p class=\"note\">In the Attack/Cast Phase you can cast any spell you can pay for. In the Block Phase only a few spells are allowed, as in the original games: Polymorph, Fog and Energize All, and two more in Etherlords II. Each card that allows it says so in its description.</p>", "search": "turn and phases a turn belongs to one hero, the attacker. it has two phases: the attacker's attack/cast phase, then the opponent's block phase. then the heroes swap roles and the next turn begins. use the end of phase button, ctrl+e in etherlords, or tab in etherlords ii to finish your phase. space skips the current presentation. start of the turn at the start of the attacker's turn, in this order: ether disturbance damage, once it has started. healing from the regeneration skill. the attacker's creatures recover (stand up) and stop being newly summoned, except under spells that keep them down. the attacker draws a spell. ether channels grow and ether is refilled to the number of channels. enchantments that act at the start of the turn take effect. attack/cast phase the attacker casts spells and chooses which ready creatures attack. creatures that rest, were just summoned (without haste) or cannot attack stay back. berserk creatures must attack. when the attacker ends the phase, the chosen creatures attack. if no creature attacks, the block phase is skipped. block phase now the defender acts: they assign blockers (and may cast the few spells allowed in this phase) to the attacking creatures. each ready creature can block one attacker, and several creatures can block the same attacker. when the defender ends the phase, combat damage is dealt: see attack and defence. end of the turn the attacker discards down to 5 spells in hand. damage on all creatures heals. enchantments that act at the end of the turn take effect. who begins in a duel, a coin decides who attacks first in every round. in campaign battles the map decides, as in the original games. in the attack/cast phase you can cast any spell you can pay for. in the block phase only a few spells are allowed, as in the original games: polymorph, fog and energize all, and two more in etherlords ii. each card that allows it says so in its description."},
		{"key": "attack-defence", "title": "Attack and defence", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>Attack and defence</h1><p>Creatures fight in the <a href=\"wiki://combat-phases\">phases</a> of a turn: the attacker chooses attackers in the Attack/Cast Phase, the defender chooses blockers in the Block Phase, and then damage is dealt.</p><h2>Who can attack</h2><p>A creature can attack if it is ready (not resting) and was not summoned this turn, unless it has <strong>Haste</strong>. These cannot attack:</p><ul><li>creatures that <strong>cannot attack</strong>, such as walls;</li><li>creatures held back by battlefield spells, for example spells that stop weak creatures;</li><li>in Etherlords II, creatures in their <strong>Pale aspect</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Berserk</strong> creatures attack whenever they can and cannot be withdrawn. A <strong>Team attacker</strong> attacks only together with a creature of its own race. Attacking creatures rest afterwards unless they are <strong>Restless</strong>.</p><h2>Blocking</h2><p>In the Block Phase every ready defending creature may block one attacker, and several creatures can block the same attacker, in the order you assign them. Limits:</p><ul><li>only a <strong>Flying</strong> creature can block a flying creature;</li><li>an <strong>Unblockable</strong> creature cannot be blocked;</li><li>an <strong>Elusive</strong> creature (Etherlords II) can only be blocked by walls.</li></ul><h2>Damage</h2><ul><li>An unblocked attacker deals its power to the defending hero.</li><li>A blocked attacker shares its power among its blockers in order, giving each as much as it needs to be destroyed before moving on to the next.</li><li>Every blocker strikes back with its own power.</li><li>A creature with <strong>First Strike</strong> hits first; if its opponent is destroyed, the opponent does not hit back.</li><li>A <strong>Trample</strong> attacker sends the damage left over after destroying its blockers on to the hero.</li></ul><p>Damage on surviving creatures heals at the end of the turn. A hero's specialisation can add chance-based effects in combat; the hero's <strong>Luck</strong> and the opponent's <strong>Suppression</strong> change the odds.</p><p>See <a href=\"wiki://abilities\">Creature abilities</a> for more abilities.</p>", "search": "attack and defence creatures fight in the phases of a turn: the attacker chooses attackers in the attack/cast phase, the defender chooses blockers in the block phase, and then damage is dealt. who can attack a creature can attack if it is ready (not resting) and was not summoned this turn, unless it has haste. these cannot attack: creatures that cannot attack, such as walls; creatures held back by battlefield spells, for example spells that stop weak creatures; in etherlords ii, creatures in their pale aspect. berserk creatures attack whenever they can and cannot be withdrawn. a team attacker attacks only together with a creature of its own race. attacking creatures rest afterwards unless they are restless. blocking in the block phase every ready defending creature may block one attacker, and several creatures can block the same attacker, in the order you assign them. limits: only a flying creature can block a flying creature; an unblockable creature cannot be blocked; an elusive creature (etherlords ii) can only be blocked by walls. damage an unblocked attacker deals its power to the defending hero. a blocked attacker shares its power among its blockers in order, giving each as much as it needs to be destroyed before moving on to the next. every blocker strikes back with its own power. a creature with first strike hits first; if its opponent is destroyed, the opponent does not hit back. a trample attacker sends the damage left over after destroying its blockers on to the hero. damage on surviving creatures heals at the end of the turn. a hero's specialisation can add chance-based effects in combat; the hero's luck and the opponent's suppression change the odds. see creature abilities for more abilities."},
		{"key": "abilities", "title": "Creature abilities", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>Creature abilities</h1><p>Abilities are shown as icons on a creature; hold the pointer over an icon for its name. The table lists the most common ones under their names in the games. Numbers in brackets vary by creature.</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Ability</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Flying</td><td>Can be blocked only by flying creatures.</td></tr><tr><td>Cannot attack</td><td>Walls: they defend but never attack.</td></tr><tr><td>First Strike</td><td>Hits before its opponent, in attack and in defence.</td></tr><tr><td>Regeneration (1–3)</td><td>When destroyed, can come back by paying the given cost.</td></tr><tr><td>Trample</td><td>Damage left after destroying its blockers goes to the enemy hero.</td></tr><tr><td>Lifetap</td><td>Damage to the enemy hero heals its owner.</td></tr><tr><td>Restless</td><td>Does not rest after attacking.</td></tr><tr><td>Disenchant</td><td>Removes the enchantments of creatures it hits.</td></tr><tr><td>Creature cannot get up</td><td>Does not recover by itself after resting.</td></tr><tr><td>Berserk</td><td>Must attack whenever it can.</td></tr><tr><td>Ether Defense</td><td>Cannot be the target of spells or abilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Unblockable</td><td>Cannot be blocked.</td></tr><tr><td>Haste</td><td>Can act on the turn it is summoned.</td></tr><tr><td>Elusive</td><td>Can be blocked only by walls.</td></tr><tr><td>Team attacker</td><td>Attacks only together with a creature of its race.</td></tr><tr><td>Poisonous</td><td>Destroys a creature it damages in combat.</td></tr><tr><td>Discard (1–3)</td><td>When it damages the enemy hero, the opponent discards that many random spells.</td></tr><tr><td>Pale ability, Pale aspect</td><td>Etherlords II: the creature can pass into its pale aspect, where it neither attacks nor is attacked.</td></tr><tr><td>Spell jamming</td><td>Etherlords II: hinders the opponent's spells.</td></tr></table><p>Many creatures also have abilities you use by resting them or paying ether; their own descriptions explain them in the game. See <a href=\"wiki://attack-defence\">Attack and defence</a> for how the combat abilities work together.</p>", "search": "creature abilities abilities are shown as icons on a creature; hold the pointer over an icon for its name. the table lists the most common ones under their names in the games. numbers in brackets vary by creature. flying can be blocked only by flying creatures. cannot attack walls: they defend but never attack. first strike hits before its opponent, in attack and in defence. regeneration (1–3) when destroyed, can come back by paying the given cost. trample damage left after destroying its blockers goes to the enemy hero. lifetap damage to the enemy hero heals its owner. restless does not rest after attacking. disenchant removes the enchantments of creatures it hits. creature cannot get up does not recover by itself after resting. berserk must attack whenever it can. ether defense cannot be the target of spells or abilities. unblockable cannot be blocked. haste can act on the turn it is summoned. elusive can be blocked only by walls. team attacker attacks only together with a creature of its race. poisonous destroys a creature it damages in combat. discard (1–3) when it damages the enemy hero, the opponent discards that many random spells. pale ability, pale aspect etherlords ii: the creature can pass into its pale aspect, where it neither attacks nor is attacked. spell jamming etherlords ii: hinders the opponent's spells. many creatures also have abilities you use by resting them or paying ether; their own descriptions explain them in the game. see attack and defence for how the combat abilities work together."},
		{"key": "automatic-combat", "title": "Automatic combat and the camera", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>Automatic combat and the camera</h1><h2>Automatic combat</h2><p>The <strong>Auto Combat</strong> button lets the computer play your side. It uses the same planning as computer opponents. Press the button again to take over. If the original option for spells in automatic combat is turned off, it casts no spells or artifacts. Automatic combat is not offered in hot seat duels or in the recorded combat tutorial.</p><h2>The battle camera</h2><p>Drag with the right mouse button to turn the camera and use the wheel to zoom; <strong>R</strong> resets the view. On touch screens, drag with one finger and pinch to zoom.</p><p>The <strong>Automatic camera</strong> shows spells, the first blow of a fight and the winner in close-ups along the games' own camera tracks before returning to your view. <strong>Automatic camera dynamics</strong> in <a href=\"wiki://options\">Options</a> sets how often close-ups happen. Moving the camera yourself ends a close-up at once. The numbers over creatures can be hidden during close-ups.</p><h2>Watching battles again</h2><p>Etherlords II <a href=\"wiki://duel\">duels</a> can be recorded and watched again from the Duel screen.</p>", "search": "automatic combat and the camera automatic combat the auto combat button lets the computer play your side. it uses the same planning as computer opponents. press the button again to take over. if the original option for spells in automatic combat is turned off, it casts no spells or artifacts. automatic combat is not offered in hot seat duels or in the recorded combat tutorial. the battle camera drag with the right mouse button to turn the camera and use the wheel to zoom; r resets the view. on touch screens, drag with one finger and pinch to zoom. the automatic camera shows spells, the first blow of a fight and the winner in close-ups along the games' own camera tracks before returning to your view. automatic camera dynamics in options sets how often close-ups happen. moving the camera yourself ends a close-up at once. the numbers over creatures can be hidden during close-ups. watching battles again etherlords ii duels can be recorded and watched again from the duel screen."},
		{"key": "adventure-map", "title": "The adventure map", "category": "rules", "html": "<h1>The adventure map</h1><p>Between battles your heroes travel the adventure map: they visit buildings, collect resources and meet enemies. Touching an enemy hero or creature starts a <a href=\"wiki://combat\">battle</a>.</p><h2>Etherlords</h2><ul><li>Each player has a <strong>castle</strong>. Its energy is the player's life: a ruined castle is out of the game. Heroes next to an enemy castle damage it every night.</li><li>Orders are given during the day and carried out at night. <strong>N</strong> ends the turn.</li><li><strong>Resources</strong>: Mandrake Root, Black Lotus, Bloody Ruby, Poison Emerald, Star Sapphire, Smoke Diamond and Frozen Flames, from their glades, mines and glaciers. <strong>Ether nodes</strong> give ether every turn; ether pays for <strong>global spells</strong> and the upkeep of heroes, and unused ether repairs the castle.</li><li><strong>Buildings</strong>: Rune Shops, Spell Shops and creature shops of three levels that can be captured; Teachers; springs such as the Spring of Life; merchant wagons; prisons; altars; Forts; and more.</li><li>Five difficulty levels: Easy, Normal, Hard, Expert and Impossible.</li></ul><h2>Etherlords II</h2><ul><li>The game runs by days; a hero can travel a limited distance each day. <strong>N</strong> ends the day.</li><li><strong>Resources</strong>: Plants, Stones and Frozen Flames.</li><li><strong>Buildings</strong>: spell shops, Sorcerer's Labs, Beacons, power nodes, specialisation shrines, Prisons, rune barriers and Forts.</li><li>Crossing the <strong>Pale lands</strong> costs heroes experience.</li><li>The bag screen holds the hero's 16 spells, at most 4 copies of one.</li><li>Three difficulty levels: Easy, Normal and Hard.</li></ul><h2>Saving</h2><p>Save with the original menus or <strong>F5</strong>; load with <strong>F9</strong>. See <a href=\"wiki://main-menu\">Main menu, saves and settings</a>.</p>", "search": "the adventure map between battles your heroes travel the adventure map: they visit buildings, collect resources and meet enemies. touching an enemy hero or creature starts a battle. etherlords each player has a castle. its energy is the player's life: a ruined castle is out of the game. heroes next to an enemy castle damage it every night. orders are given during the day and carried out at night. n ends the turn. resources: mandrake root, black lotus, bloody ruby, poison emerald, star sapphire, smoke diamond and frozen flames, from their glades, mines and glaciers. ether nodes give ether every turn; ether pays for global spells and the upkeep of heroes, and unused ether repairs the castle. buildings: rune shops, spell shops and creature shops of three levels that can be captured; teachers; springs such as the spring of life; merchant wagons; prisons; altars; forts; and more. five difficulty levels: easy, normal, hard, expert and impossible. etherlords ii the game runs by days; a hero can travel a limited distance each day. n ends the day. resources: plants, stones and frozen flames. buildings: spell shops, sorcerer's labs, beacons, power nodes, specialisation shrines, prisons, rune barriers and forts. crossing the pale lands costs heroes experience. the bag screen holds the hero's 16 spells, at most 4 copies of one. three difficulty levels: easy, normal and hard. saving save with the original menus or f5; load with f9. see main menu, saves and settings."},
		{"key": "story-world", "title": "The world of ether", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>The world of ether</h1><p>This page describes the world in which both games are set, as their manuals, briefings and dialogues present it. The plots are on <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>; the races on <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><h2>Ether</h2><p>Everything in the world, living or not, is made of <strong>ether</strong>, which the manual also calls simply magic. <strong>White ether</strong> boils at the centre of the universe. It is the strongest of all, but unstable, and it breaks down into four lesser, coloured ethers:</p><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Colour</th><th>Etherlords</th><th>Etherlords II</th></tr><tr><td>red</td><td>Chaos</td><td>ether of Fire</td></tr><tr><td>blue</td><td>Motion</td><td>ether of Air</td></tr><tr><td>green</td><td>Vitality</td><td>ether of Nature</td></tr><tr><td>black</td><td>Synthesis</td><td>ether of Technology</td></tr></table><p>Each of the four intelligent races is bound to one colour, and so are the creatures that serve it. Some simple creatures have magic of their own but cannot grow beyond what their kind can do. Heroes draw white ether only in battle, through ether channels (see <a href=\"wiki://combat\">How a battle works</a>).</p><h2>Lords and heroes</h2><p>Each race is ruled by its <strong>Lord</strong> (the English manual also says Etherlord). Lords do not fight in person. They summon <strong>heroes</strong>, who build castles, tame creatures, dig mines, open portals and study magic. Heroes never fight with weapons, only with sorceries, enchantments and summoned creatures.</p><h2>The White Lord and the Temple of Time</h2><p>At the edge of the world stands the <strong>Temple of Time</strong>, the seat of the <strong>White Lord</strong>, master of white ether and, in Etherlords II, the \"keeper of balance\" of the world. Legend says the first Lord to enter the Temple becomes the new White Lord, and the old one is banished to a distant, hostile world.</p><h2>The Time of Changes</h2><p>Roughly once in a thousand years the heavenly bodies line up with the centre of the universe (the Etherlords II texts speak of four suns) and the coloured ethers flow back together into white. The world is remade: mountains fall, rivers dry up, new lands rise and even magic changes. Barriers that kept the races apart fail, and the road to the Temple of Time opens, giving every Lord a chance at the White Lord's throne.</p><p>The flood of ether has side effects. Wild animals grow enraged, the dead rise, and ghostly beings form where flows of ether cross; these are the monsters that guard mines and treasures on the <a href=\"wiki://adventure-map\">adventure map</a>. Etherlords II adds that the altars found on the map have appeared since the last Time of Change.</p><h2>Ancient history</h2><p>The first game's briefings mention a primeval <strong>Time of Beginnings</strong>, from which the Vitals' Book of Life dates, and the <strong>Wheel of Time</strong>, which a key from the Lost City could stop and turn the other way. Long ago the Synthets attacked the Vital capital, Chebokiana; the Vitals answered with a forbidden ritual that split the mountains and buried the city, and it became the Lost City.</p><h2>The Pale and the Outer Plane</h2><p>Etherlords II adds a fifth force. The <strong>Pale</strong> (also called colourless) creatures come from the <strong>Outer Plane</strong>, another world beyond a portal that the White Lord opened. They drain ether and colour from everything they touch: a hero trapped among them weakens day by day, and pale land saps a hero's experience. Each race has its own name for the disease: the Kinets call it the Pale Plague, the Synthets the Pale Rust. Pale spells fit any spellbook (see <a href=\"wiki://races\">The four races</a>).</p>", "search": "the world of ether this page