h and mechanism, in which everything rational is allowed. their lord's commandments are quoted as law. in the first game their chief oracle doubted that the white lord existed at all. others. all other races are barbarians to them, and the chaots are also valuable experimental material. everyone else finds them sinister. alliances and enmities in the first game's time of changes the races paired off: chaots with synthets (iron and fury) and vitals with kinets (life and wisdom). both alliances broke at the gates of the temple. chaots and kinets are old enemies, as are vitals and synthets. in the single missions any pairing is possible. see the story of etherlords. other creatures neutral creatures roam everywhere: mountain orcs, wild ogres, kobolds, cyclopes, dragons, treants, crazed bees and nests of ticks. hermits such as rulana sell spells, and old buildings recall ancient heroes. in etherlords ii the pale creatures come from beyond the world; they have no will of their own and drain the ether of everything around them."},
		{"key": "chaots", "title": "Chaots", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Chaots</h1><p>The Chaots draw on the red ether: Chaos in Etherlords, the ether of Flame in Etherlords II. For how they play, see <a href=\"wiki://races\">The four races</a>; for the other races, see <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><h2>Nature and magic</h2><p>Chaots have scarlet faces like rough-hewn stone and strong, sturdy bodies. They are unruly and almost fearless, and they sweep aside whatever stands in their way. The Etherlords II manual says some call them the mightiest race; they are not, the manual adds, only the most direct: they strike first and are always at war.</p><p>Their elements are fire, lightning, lava and earthquakes. Their creatures come in aggressive packs: rats, kobolds, orcs, bats and wolves, with ogres, the <a href=\"wiki://card-cclp\">Cyclops</a> and walls of flame behind them. They have no dragons; every dragon in the game serves the Kinets.</p><h2>Society and beliefs</h2><ul><li><strong>Free warriors.</strong> Chiefs lead war-bands and meet in a great council at Jingling Rock. In Etherlords II the race is split between the <strong>West</strong> and the <strong>East</strong>, whose leaders settle disputes with duels of monsters in the Main Arena.</li><li><strong>Fire.</strong> They swear by the Flame of Chaos and the Fire of Earth. Shamans read the past and future in flames, and the <strong>Great Shaman</strong> guards the Chaos Eye, the race's holiest altar. The dead are given to the sacred flame.</li><li><strong>Law.</strong> Traitors are judged by the Law of Red Mountains. The briefings also mention the Red Star, by whose turns around the Chaot Tower of Sacraments they count time.</li><li><strong>Customs.</strong> Plunder and honour, songs of last stands, feasts, arena tournaments and beer. Their names sound Norse: Hellvard, Sofnir, Odmar Red-Fist, Alveig.</li><li><strong>Words for others.</strong> The Kinets are \"Airheads\", the Synthets \"Rusties\", the Vitals \"green slime\". To everyone else the Chaots are barbarians and fire-worshippers.</li></ul><h2>In the story</h2><ul><li><strong>Etherlords.</strong> The Chaots open the Flaming Iron campaign with the Fire Ordeal, an ancient rite proving that their warrior spirit survived the long peace. Allied with the Synthets, they fight their old enemies the Kinets, capture Diamanda for their allies and reach the Lost City, where they meet a Synthet monster. Calling it treachery, they turn on the Synthets at the gates of the Temple of Time. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</li><li><strong>Etherlords II.</strong> The young warrior Konnar of Fire Valley fights a Kinet invasion that crossed a lake on bridges of ice, sees the Great Shaman killed at the Chaos Eye and frees the City of Thundering Stones. The campaign ends in a peace with the Kinets, who pay with the Northern Island. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</li></ul><h2>Homeland and places</h2><p>The Chaots' homeland is a land of red mountains and volcanoes; the Synthets call them spawn of the fiery deserts. In Etherlords II it is the <strong>Land of Chaos</strong>, with Fire Valley, the City of Thundering Stones, its Western and Eastern Castles and Main Arena, the Island Fortress and the Chaos Eye in a volcano crater. See <a href=\"wiki://places\">Places</a>.</p><p>Some old buildings on the Etherlords II map carry Chaot magic: an active volcano, an Altar of Storms that doubles lightning spells, and a Lightning Generator that the briefing says an ancient Chaot hero built.</p><h2>Creatures and spells</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Creature</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-cclp\">Cyclops</a></td><td>A \"living catapult\"; Krogerd's Fighting Cyclops loses a monster duel to Alveig's dragon</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-ogkn\">Ogre King</a></td><td>Leader of the ogres the Chaots curse by</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-orwr\">Orc Warrior</a></td><td>The orcs fight with Orc's Rage</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-kbel\">Kobold Elder</a></td><td>Head of a kobold tribe of shamans, guards and warriors</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-dmrt\">Doom Rat</a></td><td>The last of the rat line, from Stink Rat to Doom Rat</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-vbat\">Vampire Bat</a></td><td>Grows on the taste of blood</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-wlif\">Wall of Inferno Flames</a></td><td>One of the walls of fire, lava and stone</td></tr></table><p>Their signature sorceries are direct damage: <a href=\"wiki://card-frbl\">Fireball</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-clig\">Call Lightning</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-rnst\">Rain of Stones</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-frst\">Firestorm</a>.</p><h2>Resources and rituals</h2><p>In Etherlords the Chaots' mineral is the <strong>Bloody Ruby</strong>, dug in ruby mines; their Blood Ritual produces it. Their global spells include Meteor and Chain Lightning, and their ultimate spell is the Clock of the Apocalypse. In Etherlords II all minerals became one resource, stones. The Chaot castle is the Castle of Chaos.</p>", "search": "chaots the chaots draw on the red ether: chaos in etherlords, the ether of flame in etherlords ii. for how they play, see the four races; for the other races, see races and peoples. nature and magic chaots have scarlet faces like rough-hewn stone and strong, sturdy bodies. they are unruly and almost fearless, and they sweep aside whatever stands in their way. the etherlords ii manual says some call them the mightiest race; they are not, the manual adds, only the most direct: they strike first and are always at war. their elements are fire, lightning, lava and earthquakes. their creatures come in aggressive packs: rats, kobolds, orcs, bats and wolves, with ogres, the cyclops and walls of flame behind them. they have no dragons; every dragon in the game serves the kinets. society and beliefs free warriors. chiefs lead war-bands and meet in a great council at jingling rock. in etherlords ii the race is split between the west and the east, whose leaders settle disputes with duels of monsters in the main arena. fire. they swear by the flame of chaos and the fire of earth. shamans read the past and future in flames, and the great shaman guards the chaos eye, the race's holiest altar. the dead are given to the sacred flame. law. traitors are judged by the law of red mountains. the briefings also mention the red star, by whose turns around the chaot tower of sacraments they count time. customs. plunder and honour, songs of last stands, feasts, arena tournaments and beer. their names sound norse: hellvard, sofnir, odmar red-fist, alveig. words for others. the kinets are \"airheads\", the synthets \"rusties\", the vitals \"green slime\". to everyone else the chaots are barbarians and fire-worshippers. in the story etherlords. the chaots open the flaming iron campaign with the fire ordeal, an ancient rite proving that their warrior spirit survived the long peace. allied with the synthets, they fight their old enemies the kinets, capture diamanda for their allies and reach the lost city, where they meet a synthet monster. calling it treachery, they turn on the synthets at the gates of the temple of time. see the story of etherlords. etherlords ii. the young warrior konnar of fire valley fights a kinet invasion that crossed a lake on bridges of ice, sees the great shaman killed at the chaos eye and frees the city of thundering stones. the campaign ends in a peace with the kinets, who pay with the northern island. see the story of etherlords ii. homeland and places the chaots' homeland is a land of red mountains and volcanoes; the synthets call them spawn of the fiery deserts. in etherlords ii it is the land of chaos, with fire valley, the city of thundering stones, its western and eastern castles and main arena, the island fortress and the chaos eye in a volcano crater. see places. some old buildings on the etherlords ii map carry chaot magic: an active volcano, an altar of storms that doubles lightning spells, and a lightning generator that the briefing says an ancient chaot hero built. creatures and spells cyclops a \"living catapult\"; krogerd's fighting cyclops loses a monster duel to alveig's dragon ogre king leader of the ogres the chaots curse by orc warrior the orcs fight with orc's rage kobold elder head of a kobold tribe of shamans, guards and warriors doom rat the last of the rat line, from stink rat to doom rat vampire bat grows on the taste of blood wall of inferno flames one of the walls of fire, lava and stone their signature sorceries are direct damage: fireball, call lightning, rain of stones and firestorm. resources and rituals in etherlords the chaots' mineral is the bloody ruby, dug in ruby mines; their blood ritual produces it. their global spells include meteor and chain lightning, and their ultimate spell is the clock of the apocalypse. in etherlords ii all minerals became one resource, stones. the chaot castle is the castle of chaos."},
		{"key": "kinets", "title": "Kinets", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Kinets</h1><p>The Kinets draw on the blue ether: Motion in Etherlords, the ether of Air in Etherlords II. For how they play, see <a href=\"wiki://races\">The four races</a>; for the other races, see <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><h2>Nature and magic</h2><p>The Kinets are the most intellectual race, the \"winged philosopher race\" of Etherlords II. They have impassive faces and slender, delicate frames, and they live long lives. They value life, but knowledge more: \"Strength through knowledge.\" A Kinet hero prefers speed to brute force, parrying and striking back until the enemy is exhausted.</p><p>Their elements are water and wind, snowstorms and floods. Their allies are creatures that seek freedom and knowledge: Aviaks, Lamias, Gibberlings, halos, spirits, all four elementals and all four dragons, the mightiest creatures of the world.</p><h2>Society and beliefs</h2><ul><li><strong>Order.</strong> Their ideals are Stability and Order and the Harmony of the Spheres, which they also shout as a battle cry. In Etherlords II their castle is the Castle of Ordering, their priests serve the Temple of Order, and their age is the Era of Enlightened Harmony.</li><li><strong>The Council of Sixteen.</strong> In Etherlords II the Kinets are ruled by a council whose members are called the Betters. Its head, addressed as Blessed One, was Dominicius. Other offices include the Keeper of Keys.</li><li><strong>Knowledge.</strong> The first game mentions the Vault of True Knowledge, with documents from the Beginning of Time, and a yearly tournament in an ice labyrinth whose winner may speak with the Greatest Teacher.</li><li><strong>Customs.</strong> Bows, formal invitations, \"noble\" and \"honourable\" forms of address, and fine calligraphy. The seer Droncan speaks in verse.</li><li><strong>Words for others.</strong> They call the Synthets rusty scum and the Chaots barbarians; the Synthets call them \"Pneumatics\" and the Chaots \"Airheads\".</li></ul><h2>In the story</h2><ul><li><strong>Etherlords.</strong> Allied with the Vitals in the Flying Leaf campaign, the Kinets find the way to the Lost City through an ancient tomb. Their heroine <strong>Diamanda</strong>, Mistress of Dragons, is captured by the Chaots for the Synthets. Accused by the Vitals of giving her up, they turn on their ally at the gates of the Temple. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</li><li><strong>Etherlords II.</strong> The Pale Plague eats Kinet lands and drives them to invade the Chaots. The young messenger Mirabell frees the noble Rinalda from Diamanda's trap, uncovers a plot against the Head of the Sixteen, performs a rite that weakens the plague, and faces Diamanda on the northern peninsula. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</li></ul><h2>Homeland and places</h2><p>The Lands of Movement lie by the sea, with islands, canyons and ice. Their capital, the <strong>City of Eight Gates</strong>, is a thousand years old. Other places include the Market Place, Fish Island, Snake Canyon, the Temple of Order and the Shrines of Order, which shield the Kinet lands from hostile magic. The <strong>northern peninsula</strong>, where the White Lord's portal opens, lies on their border with the Synthets. See <a href=\"wiki://places\">Places</a>.</p><p>Old Kinet buildings on the map include a Temple of Frost Wind that sends ice storms, an Aviak shrine that drives Aviaks into a frenzy, and an Ancestral Tomb where the souls of fallen warriors return to help.</p><h2>Creatures and spells</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Creature</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-bldg\">Black Dragon</a></td><td>In Etherlords II a Kinet black dragon in Synthet lands awaits a coming \"Master\"</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-budg\">Blue Dragon</a></td><td>Guards the northern peninsula</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-rddg\">Red Dragon</a></td><td>Probably the kind of Alveig's Fighting Dragon</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-avel\">Aviak Elder</a></td><td>The Aviaks once lived under Kinet rule</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-lmwk\">Lamia Warlock</a></td><td>The Lamias form a sisterhood of monks, warriors and warlocks</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-wzsp\">Wizard Spirit</a></td><td>One of the four spirits that keep the ancient tomb</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-arel\">Air Elemental</a></td><td>All four elementals are Kinet creatures</td></tr></table><p>Their signature spells are control: <a href=\"wiki://card-icst\">Ice Storm</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-grun\">Greater Unsummoning</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-chcm\">Change of Command</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-stsi\">Stasis</a>.</p><h2>Resources and rituals</h2><p>In Etherlords the Kinets' mineral is the <strong>Star Sapphire</strong>, dug in sapphire mines; their Star Ritual produces it. Their global spells include Shield of Ice and Hypnotism, and their ultimate spell is Ether Leak. Their castle in the first game is the Castle of Motion.</p>", "search": "kinets the kinets draw on the blue ether: motion in etherlords, the ether of air in etherlords ii. for how they play, see the four races; for the other races, see races and peoples. nature and magic the kinets are the most intellectual race, the \"winged philosopher race\" of etherlords ii. they have impassive faces and slender, delicate frames, and they live long lives. they value life, but knowledge more: \"strength through knowledge.\" a kinet hero prefers speed to brute force, parrying and striking back until the enemy is exhausted. their elements are water and wind, snowstorms and floods. their allies are creatures that seek freedom and knowledge: aviaks, lamias, gibberlings, halos, spirits, all four elementals and all four dragons, the mightiest creatures of the world. society and beliefs order. their ideals are stability and order and the harmony of the spheres, which they also shout as a battle cry. in etherlords ii their castle is the castle of ordering, their priests serve the temple of order, and their age is the era of enlightened harmony. the council of sixteen. in etherlords ii the kinets are ruled by a council whose members are called the betters. its head, addressed as blessed one, was dominicius. other offices include the keeper of keys. knowledge. the first game mentions the vault of true knowledge, with documents from the beginning of time, and a yearly tournament in an ice labyrinth whose winner may speak with the greatest teacher. customs. bows, formal invitations, \"noble\" and \"honourable\" forms of address, and fine calligraphy. the seer droncan speaks in verse. words for others. they call the synthets rusty scum and the chaots barbarians; the synthets call them \"pneumatics\" and the chaots \"airheads\". in the story etherlords. allied with the vitals in the flying leaf campaign, the kinets find the way to the lost city through an ancient tomb. their heroine diamanda, mistress of dragons, is captured by the chaots for the synthets. accused by the vitals of giving her up, they turn on their ally at the gates of the temple. see the story of etherlords. etherlords ii. the pale plague eats kinet lands and drives them to invade the chaots. the young messenger mirabell frees the noble rinalda from diamanda's trap, uncovers a plot against the head of the sixteen, performs a rite that weakens the plague, and faces diamanda on the northern peninsula. see the story of etherlords ii. homeland and places the lands of movement lie by the sea, with islands, canyons and ice. their capital, the city of eight gates, is a thousand years old. other places include the market place, fish island, snake canyon, the temple of order and the shrines of order, which shield the kinet lands from hostile magic. the northern peninsula, where the white lord's portal opens, lies on their border with the synthets. see places. old kinet buildings on the map include a temple of frost wind that sends ice storms, an aviak shrine that drives aviaks into a frenzy, and an ancestral tomb where the souls of fallen warriors return to help. creatures and spells black dragon in etherlords ii a kinet black dragon in synthet lands awaits a coming \"master\" blue dragon guards the northern peninsula red dragon probably the kind of alveig's fighting dragon aviak elder the aviaks once lived under kinet rule lamia warlock the lamias form a sisterhood of monks, warriors and warlocks wizard spirit one of the four spirits that keep the ancient tomb air elemental all four elementals are kinet creatures their signature spells are control: ice storm, greater unsummoning, change of command and stasis. resources and rituals in etherlords the kinets' mineral is the star sapphire, dug in sapphire mines; their star ritual produces it. their global spells include shield of ice and hypnotism, and their ultimate spell is ether leak. their castle in the first game is the castle of motion."},
		{"key": "vitals", "title": "Vitals", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Vitals</h1><p>The Vitals draw on the green ether: Vitality in Etherlords, the ether of Nature in Etherlords II. For how they play, see <a href=\"wiki://races\">The four races</a>; for the other races, see <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><h2>Nature and magic</h2><p>The Vitals are children of nature, a beautiful race that lives in green forests by murmuring rivers. They are attuned to nature at its most basic level and use the magic of animals and plants like no other race; nature herself takes their side. Their prime element is the eternal cycle of life and death.</p><p>Their allies come from forest, field and moor, \"from ticks to snakes\": treants, bees, mantises, finguses and the many-eyed Evil Eyes. In battle they balance attack with a strong defence.</p><h2>Society and beliefs</h2><ul><li><strong>Clans.</strong> Vitals live in clans named after plants, such as the Rowan, Blackthorn and Nut-Tree clans, under the rule of elders. Heroes call each other brother and sister and pledge \"my staff is in your hands\".</li><li><strong>The Forest.</strong> They call themselves the children of the Forest and invoke the Power of the Forest. A saying: the tree is strong because it grows in the forest. They swear \"By Life\" and greet each other with \"May the Force of Life be with you\".</li><li><strong>Sacred places.</strong> The Book of Life dates from the Time of Beginnings. The Spring of Life lies on an island in the Forbidden Grove, which only initiates may enter; the uninitiated are destroyed by the Forest's power. A Keeper guards the Spring day and night.</li><li><strong>Words for others.</strong> They call the Synthets soulless \"Rusties\" and the Chaots weak of mind; to the others they are forest barbarians.</li></ul><h2>In the story</h2><ul><li><strong>Etherlords.</strong> The Lord of Vitality, who hates war and death, allies with the Kinets in the Flying Leaf campaign. The Vitals reach their own ancient capital, the Lost City, and take the Key to the Temple of Time, but break with the Kinets over Diamanda's fate. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</li><li><strong>Etherlords II.</strong> Eileen of Rowan clan faces a Synthet invasion through mad teleporters, fights in the Forbidden Grove and saves Golden Ash-Tree City. She defeats Flon, a Vital who betrayed his people to the Synthets, and realises that the legendary Diamanda has returned. Vital legend credits the heroine Ciara with destroying Diamanda long ago. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</li></ul><h2>Homeland and places</h2><p>The Forest Land is wooded country of lakes and groves. Its capital in Etherlords II is <strong>Golden Ash-Tree City</strong>, whose city dragon Musician hears the Music of the Spheres and whose walls stand as long as an ancient treant stays. Long ago the Vital capital was <strong>Chebokkiana</strong>: when the Synthets attacked it, the Vitals performed a forbidden ritual that buried the city, and it became the Lost City. See <a href=\"wiki://places\">Places</a>.</p><p>Old forest buildings on the map include a Hermit's Hut where a hermit blesses the forest, the Protector of Life, an ancient tree that avenges any creature killed nearby, and a Shrine of Nature that raises the forest against its defilers.</p><h2>Creatures and spells</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Creature</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-antn\">Ancient Treant</a></td><td>The oldest of the treants, from sapling to ancient</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-bhmo\">Evil Eye Mother</a></td><td>Mother of the Evil Eyes</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-mtpt\">Mantis Patriarch</a></td><td>The mantises include a Mantis Scribe, a chronicler</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-beqn\">Bee Queen</a></td><td>Crazed bees threaten Golden Ash-Tree City</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-moti\">Mother Tick</a></td><td>Spawns ticks; tick nests are a hazard of the forest</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-grfg\">Gargantuan Fingus</a></td><td>The largest of the spore-casting finguses</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-trsn\">Tree Snake</a></td><td>In the first game, a gift of the forest that joins every battle</td></tr></table><p>Their signature spells call on the forest: <a href=\"wiki://card-frnt\">Fury of Nature</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-anfr\">Ancient Forest</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-twst\">Twister</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-aslt\">Assault</a>.</p><h2>Resources and rituals</h2><p>In Etherlords the Vitals' mineral is the <strong>Poison Emerald</strong>, dug in emerald mines; their Snake Ritual produces it. Their global spells include Gifts of the Forest and Blessing of the Forest, and their ultimate spell, Ether Calm, stops ether combat altogether. Their castle is the Castle of Vitality in the first game and the Castle of Life in the second.</p>", "search": "vitals the vitals draw on the green ether: vitality in etherlords, the ether of nature in etherlords ii. for how they play, see the four races; for the other races, see races and peoples. nature and magic the vitals are children of nature, a beautiful race that lives in green forests by murmuring rivers. they are attuned to nature at its most basic level and use the magic of animals and plants like no other race; nature herself takes their side. their prime element is the eternal cycle of life and death. their allies come from forest, field and moor, \"from ticks to snakes\": treants, bees, mantises, finguses and the many-eyed evil eyes. in battle they balance attack with a strong defence. society and beliefs clans. vitals live in clans named after plants, such as the rowan, blackthorn and nut-tree clans, under the rule of elders. heroes call each other brother and sister and pledge \"my staff is in your hands\". the forest. they call themselves the children of the forest and invoke the power of the forest. a saying: the tree is strong because it grows in the forest. they swear \"by life\" and greet each other with \"may the force of life be with you\". sacred places. the book of life dates from the time of beginnings. the spring of life lies on an island in the forbidden grove, which only initiates may enter; the uninitiated are destroyed by the forest's power. a keeper guards the spring day and night. words for others. they call the synthets soulless \"rusties\" and the chaots weak of mind; to the others they are forest barbarians. in the story etherlords. the lord of vitality, who hates war and death, allies with the kinets in the flying leaf campaign. the vitals reach their own ancient capital, the lost city, and take the key to the temple of time, but break with the kinets over diamanda's fate. see the story of etherlords. etherlords ii. eileen of rowan clan faces a synthet invasion through mad teleporters, fights in the forbidden grove and saves golden ash-tree city. she defeats flon, a vital who betrayed his people to the synthets, and realises that the legendary diamanda has returned. vital legend credits the heroine ciara with destroying diamanda long ago. see the story of etherlords ii. homeland and places the forest land is wooded country of lakes and groves. its capital in etherlords ii is golden ash-tree city, whose city dragon musician hears the music of the spheres and whose walls stand as long as an ancient treant stays. long ago the vital capital was chebokkiana: when the synthets attacked it, the vitals performed a forbidden ritual that buried the city, and it became the lost city. see places. old forest buildings on the map include a hermit's hut where a hermit blesses the forest, the protector of life, an ancient tree that avenges any creature killed nearby, and a shrine of nature that raises the forest against its defilers. creatures and spells ancient treant the oldest of the treants, from sapling to ancient evil eye mother mother of the evil eyes mantis patriarch the mantises include a mantis scribe, a chronicler bee queen crazed bees threaten golden ash-tree city mother tick spawns ticks; tick nests are a hazard of the forest gargantuan fingus the largest of the spore-casting finguses tree snake in the first game, a gift of the forest that joins every battle their signature spells call on the forest: fury of nature, ancient forest, twister and assault. resources and rituals in etherlords the vitals' mineral is the poison emerald, dug in emerald mines; their snake ritual produces it. their global spells include gifts of the forest and blessing of the forest, and their ultimate spell, ether calm, stops ether combat altogether. their castle is the castle of vitality in the first game and the castle of life in the second."},
		{"key": "synthets", "title": "Synthets", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Synthets</h1><p>The Synthets draw on the black ether: Synthesis in Etherlords, the ether of the Machine in Etherlords II. For how they play, see <a href=\"wiki://races\">The four races</a>; for the other races, see <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><h2>Nature and magic</h2><p>The Synthets replace most of their bodies with machinery, keeping a minimum of living matter, which gives them great endurance. The Etherlords II campaign calls them a fusion of mortal and machine. Their implants are grown over generations, and a hero's own will holds them together: \"Synthesis is not a gift, it's a burden.\"</p><p>Their magic dissolves the barriers between living and dead matter, reality and illusion, time and information. Their creatures are pseudo-machines: mechanical worms, hoppers, Veloses on wheels, mechozauruses and pitiless mechanical cutters.</p><h2>Society and beliefs</h2><ul><li><strong>Designations.</strong> Heroes bear model codes rather than names: a Greek letter, a number and a nickname, such as Alpha-100F \"Steel\" or Beta-600F \"Hammer\".</li><li><strong>Perfection.</strong> Their goal is the Path to Perfection, in Etherlords II the <strong>Great Synthesis</strong>: cruel and bold experiments joining flesh and mechanism, in which everything rational is allowed. They speak of production targets and upgrades.</li><li><strong>The Lord's commandments.</strong> Heroes quote their Lord as law: Synthesis guards those who serve it; accept treachery, then get rid of the traitor.</li><li><strong>The Cluster.</strong> In Etherlords II a networked intelligence broadcasts orders and breaks codes, and coordinators run the cities. In the first game a Chief Oracle analysed the information spread in the ether, and concluded that the White Lord might be only a fluctuation of information.</li><li><strong>Words for others.</strong> All other races are barbarians to them; the Kinets are \"Pneumatics\", and the Chaots valuable experimental material. Everyone else finds the Synthets sinister and calls them \"Rusties\".</li></ul><h2>In the story</h2><ul><li><strong>Etherlords.</strong> The Lord of Synthesis proposes the alliance of Iron and Fury to the Chaots. The Synthets ask their allies to capture the Kinet heroine Diamanda for their rituals, and their scientists turn her into a Synthet warrior. They defeat the Fire Konung for an element they need, and at the gates of the Temple simply decide to go on alone. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</li><li><strong>Etherlords II.</strong> The <strong>Pale Rust</strong> consumes the Synthets' northern lands and then their capital. The soldier Alpha frees the hero Beta from Diamanda's trap, uses the Vital traitor Flon to break into a Vital fortress, destroys the pale monster that drains Axis City and faces Diamanda on the northern peninsula. Their scientists learn pale magic of their own. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</li></ul><h2>Homeland and places</h2><p>The Land of Synthesis lies in the north. Its capital, <strong>Axis City</strong>, is lost to the Pale Rust in Etherlords II, and its Shrine of Synthesis shields it from hostile magic. The Synthets rest at ether nodes, which the Rust cannot touch. See <a href=\"wiki://places\">Places</a>.</p><p>Synthet engineering survives in old buildings: a Mech Worms facility built in ancient times, a Disturbance Generator once used for magic experiments, and the Purify Station, a Synthet crystal that turns their Purify spell into a weapon against the Pale.</p><h2>Creatures and spells</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Creature</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-wkhr\">Walking Horror</a></td><td>The monster of the Lost City, and again in Etherlords II</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-rzmn\">Razor Man</a></td><td>The last of the cutters, after the Cutter Man and Sawer Man</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-stmz\">Steel Mechozaurus</a></td><td>The mechozaurus comes in bronze, iron and steel</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-mcwy\">Mech Wyrm</a></td><td>The largest of the mechanical worms</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-bgvl\">Big Velos</a></td><td>The wheeled Veloses</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-rfpt\">Reinforced Pteros</a></td><td>Armoured flyer with a piercing gaze</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"wiki://card-rdkn\">Konung</a></td><td>Artifact creature summoned with a rod; in Russian the item is the Rod of Konung</td></tr></table><p>Their signature spells destroy and drain: <a href=\"wiki://card-mdwp\">Mindwipe</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-lqrl\">Steal Reality</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-alst\">Alpha Strike</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-prfy\">Purify</a>, the Synthets' answer to the Pale.</p><h2>Resources and rituals</h2><p>In Etherlords the Synthets' mineral is the <strong>Smoke Diamond</strong>, dug in diamond mines; their Smoke Ritual produces it. Their global spells include Biohazard and Channel Warp, and their ultimate spell is Terminal Discharge. Their castle is the Castle of Synthesis.</p>", "search": "synthets the synthets draw on the black ether: synthesis in etherlords, the ether of the machine in etherlords ii. for how they play, see the four races; for the other races, see races and peoples. nature and magic the synthets replace most of their bodies with machinery, keeping a minimum of living matter, which gives them great endurance. the etherlords ii campaign calls them a fusion of mortal and machine. their implants are grown over generations, and a hero's own will holds them together: \"synthesis is not a gift, it's a burden.\" their magic dissolves the barriers between living and dead matter, reality and illusion, time and information. their creatures are pseudo-machines: mechanical worms, hoppers, veloses on wheels, mechozauruses and pitiless mechanical cutters. society and beliefs designations. heroes bear model codes rather than names: a greek letter, a number and a nickname, such as alpha-100f \"steel\" or beta-600f \"hammer\". perfection. their goal is the path to perfection, in etherlords ii the great synthesis: cruel and bold experiments joining flesh and mechanism, in which everything rational is allowed. they speak of production targets and upgrades. the lord's commandments. heroes quote their lord as law: synthesis guards those who serve it; accept treachery, then get rid of the traitor. the cluster. in etherlords ii a networked intelligence broadcasts orders and breaks codes, and coordinators run the cities. in the first game a chief oracle analysed the information spread in the ether, and concluded that the white lord might be only a fluctuation of information. words for others. all other races are barbarians to them; the kinets are \"pneumatics\", and the chaots valuable experimental material. everyone else finds the synthets sinister and calls them \"rusties\". in the story etherlords. the lord of synthesis proposes the alliance of iron and fury to the chaots. the synthets ask their allies to capture the kinet heroine diamanda for their rituals, and their scientists turn her into a synthet warrior. they defeat the fire konung for an element they need, and at the gates of the temple simply decide to go on alone. see the story of etherlords. etherlords ii. the pale rust consumes the synthets' northern lands and then their capital. the soldier alpha frees the hero beta from diamanda's trap, uses the vital traitor flon to break into a vital fortress, destroys the pale monster that drains axis city and faces diamanda on the northern peninsula. their scientists learn pale magic of their own. see the story of etherlords ii. homeland and places the land of synthesis lies in the north. its capital, axis city, is lost to the pale rust in etherlords ii, and its shrine of synthesis shields it from hostile magic. the synthets rest at ether nodes, which the rust cannot touch. see places. synthet engineering survives in old buildings: a mech worms facility built in ancient times, a disturbance generator once used for magic experiments, and the purify station, a synthet crystal that turns their purify spell into a weapon against the pale. creatures and spells walking horror the monster of the lost city, and again in etherlords ii razor man the last of the cutters, after the cutter man and sawer man steel mechozaurus the mechozaurus comes in bronze, iron and steel mech wyrm the largest of the mechanical worms big velos the wheeled veloses reinforced pteros armoured flyer with a piercing gaze konung artifact creature summoned with a rod; in russian the item is the rod of konung their signature spells destroy and drain: mindwipe, steal reality, alpha strike and purify, the synthets' answer to the pale. resources and rituals in etherlords the synthets' mineral is the smoke diamond, dug in diamond mines; their smoke ritual produces it. their global spells include biohazard and channel warp, and their ultimate spell is terminal discharge. their castle is the castle of synthesis."},
		{"key": "pale", "title": "The Pale", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>The Pale</h1><p>The <strong>Pale</strong> (also called colourless) are the new force of Etherlords II. In the story they are invaders from another world; in play they are a fifth, colourless group of creatures and spells that any hero may use. See <a href=\"wiki://story-world\">The world of ether</a> for the background.</p><h2>Where they come from</h2><p>The Pale come from the <strong>Outer Plane</strong>, a world beyond an ancient sealed portal. The White Lord sacrificed the souls of mighty heroes to open it on the northern peninsula, and only his will keeps it open. He meant the Pale to drain the flows of ether so that the races would become docile pale shadows and no new Lord could ever take his throne. See <a href=\"wiki://white-lord\">The White Lord</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p><p>On their own the Pale are mindless creatures without a will. Diamanda can command them, and late in the story Synthet scientists learn to summon them too.</p><h2>The plague</h2><p>Wherever the Pale spread, colour and ether drain from the world. Each race names the disease in its own way:</p><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Race</th><th>Name</th><th>What they believe</th></tr><tr><td>Kinets</td><td>the Pale Plague</td><td>the Synthets carry it</td></tr><tr><td>Synthets</td><td>the Pale Rust</td><td>their neighbours spread it; one scientist thinks it punishes them for loving upgrades more than their Lord's commandments</td></tr><tr><td>Vitals</td><td>the pale disease, pale beasts</td><td>Synthet machines that turned on their makers</td></tr></table><p>A hero trapped among them loses strength day by day until the hero becomes colourless, feeble and meek; the Synthet hero Beta felt it as lost synchronisation and failing implants. Powerful pale monsters act as funnels through which ether is sucked out of the world. The Synthets found that their ether nodes resist the Rust.</p><h2>Pale lands</h2><p>On some Etherlords II maps whole regions turn grey. A hero walking in this <strong>pale land</strong> loses experience, which can even lower the hero's level; the in-game text says lost experience is recovered at ether sources. The scripts widen or shrink the pale zone from chapter to chapter as the story goes on.</p><h2>Pale and bright</h2><p>Pale creatures exist in two <strong>aspects</strong>: pale, when they are not really in the world, and bright, when they have manifested. Each family pays its own price to manifest:</p><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Family</th><th>Creatures</th><th>Price to manifest</th></tr><tr><td>Warriors</td><td><a href=\"wiki://card-smfx\">Pale Soldier</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-smtg\">Pale Zealot</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-smwb\">Pale Knight</a></td><td>exile a creature from a graveyard</td></tr><tr><td>Reapers</td><td><a href=\"wiki://card-oud1\">Pale Scourge</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-oud2\">Pale Reaper</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-oud3\">Pale Slayer</a></td><td>sacrifice an ether channel</td></tr><tr><td>Weavers</td><td><a href=\"wiki://card-oue1\">Pale Spelleater</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-oue2\">Pale Spellweaver</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-oue3\">Pale Spellshifter</a></td><td>destroy one of your enchantments</td></tr><tr><td>Mages</td><td><a href=\"wiki://card-ous1\">Pale Channeler</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-ous2\">Pale Invoker</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-ous3\">Pale Healer</a></td><td>sacrifice a creature</td></tr><tr><td>Punishers</td><td><a href=\"wiki://card-osj1\">Pale Avenger</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-osj2\">Pale Revenant</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-osj3\">Pale Redeemer</a></td><td>sacrifice a spell from your hand</td></tr></table><p>The Reapers kill weaker bright creatures and grow from them. The Punishers lie in wait as enchantments and become creatures when the enemy casts a sorcery. The boss that drains Axis City is a Pale Slayer.</p><h2>Pale spells</h2><p>Pale sorceries and enchantments play on the two aspects: <a href=\"wiki://card-prwt\">Paleness</a> makes every creature enter pale, <a href=\"wiki://card-prbn\">Lock Aspect</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-plsl\">Seal</a> stop aspect changes, <a href=\"wiki://card-bnsh\">Dissipate</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-gbns\">Vaporize</a> send pale creatures away, and <a href=\"wiki://card-orpl\">Ripple</a>, <a href=\"wiki://card-oflx\">Flux</a> and <a href=\"wiki://card-obls\">Blast</a> strike them. <a href=\"wiki://card-plwp\">Pale Warp</a> lasts only while a pale creature is in play. The Synthets' <a href=\"wiki://card-prfy\">Purify</a>, strengthened by the Purify Station, destroys pale creatures outright.</p><p>The White Lord's last guardian is a colourless <a href=\"wiki://card-ekon\">Konung</a>, the same creature the Synthets summon with a rod.</p>", "search": "the pale the pale (also called colourless) are the new force of etherlords ii. in the story they are invaders from another world; in play they are a fifth, colourless group of creatures and spells that any hero may use. see the world of ether for the background. where they come from the pale come from the outer plane, a world beyond an ancient sealed portal. the white lord sacrificed the souls of mighty heroes to open it on the northern peninsula, and only his will keeps it open. he meant the pale to drain the flows of ether so that the races would become docile pale shadows and no new lord could ever take his throne. see the white lord and the story of etherlords ii. on their own the pale are mindless creatures without a will. diamanda can command them, and late in the story synthet scientists learn to summon them too. the plague wherever the pale spread, colour and ether drain from the world. each race names the disease in its own way: kinets the pale plague the synthets carry it synthets the pale rust their neighbours spread it; one scientist thinks it punishes them for loving upgrades more than their lord's commandments vitals the pale disease, pale beasts synthet machines that turned on their makers a hero trapped among them loses strength day by day until the hero becomes colourless, feeble and meek; the synthet hero beta felt it as lost synchronisation and failing implants. powerful pale monsters act as funnels through which ether is sucked out of the world. the synthets found that their ether nodes resist the rust. pale lands on some etherlords ii maps whole regions turn grey. a hero walking in this pale land loses experience, which can even lower the hero's level; the in-game text says lost experience is recovered at ether sources. the scripts widen or shrink the pale zone from chapter to chapter as the story goes on. pale and bright pale creatures exist in two aspects: pale, when they are not really in the world, and bright, when they have manifested. each family pays its own price to manifest: warriors pale soldier, pale zealot, pale knight exile a creature from a graveyard reapers pale scourge, pale reaper, pale slayer sacrifice an ether channel weavers pale spelleater, pale spellweaver, pale spellshifter destroy one of your enchantments mages pale channeler, pale invoker, pale healer sacrifice a creature punishers pale avenger, pale revenant, pale redeemer sacrifice a spell from your hand the reapers kill weaker bright creatures and grow from them. the punishers lie in wait as enchantments and become creatures when the enemy casts a sorcery. the boss that drains axis city is a pale slayer. pale spells pale sorceries and enchantments play on the two aspects: paleness makes every creature enter pale, lock aspect and seal stop aspect changes, dissipate and vaporize send pale creatures away, and ripple, flux and blast strike them. pale warp lasts only while a pale creature is in play. the synthets' purify, strengthened by the purify station, destroys pale creatures outright. the white lord's last guardian is a colourless konung, the same creature the synthets summon with a rod."},
		{"key": "places", "title": "Places", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Places</h1><p>The named places of the world of ether, as the briefings, dialogues and map labels give them. The plots are on <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p><h2>The Temple of Time</h2><p>The <strong>Temple of Time</strong> stands at the edge of the world. It is the seat of the White Lord and the goal of every Lord in a Time of Changes; the first to enter it is said to become the new White Lord. In the first game the road to it opens only once a rival race is beaten, and only a Lord may take the last step inside. In Etherlords II the White Lord's castle and his <strong>Portal</strong> to the Outer Plane stand on the northern peninsula. See <a href=\"wiki://white-lord\">The White Lord</a>.</p><h2>Places of Etherlords</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Lost City</td><td>Chebokkiana, the ancient capital of the Vitals, buried by a forbidden ritual when the Synthets attacked it. In the ruins of its town centre lies the Key to the Temple of Time</td></tr><tr><td>Foggy Forest</td><td>Home of an ancient tomb guarded by ghostly Keepers; its fog brings illusions and weak sight</td></tr><tr><td>Wild Wasteland</td><td>Lair of the Fire Konung, whose strength comes from four Altars of War</td></tr><tr><td>Vault of True Knowledge</td><td>Kinet archive with documents from the Beginning of Time</td></tr><tr><td>Sacred Forest</td><td>Vital forest, which has grown and died eight times since the Lost City fell</td></tr><tr><td>Red Mountains</td><td>Chaot homeland, which gives its name to the Chaots' law</td></tr><tr><td>Wooden Cage</td><td>The prison of trees where a legendary hero waits to be freed</td></tr><tr><td>Snowflake Maze</td><td>Ice labyrinth of the yearly Kinet tournament</td></tr><tr><td>Borderlands</td><td>Where the lands of all four races meet; the road to new lands is said to begin there</td></tr></table><h2>Lands of the Vitals</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Forest Land</td><td>The Vital country</td></tr><tr><td>Blackthorn Fortress</td><td>Northern fortress of the Blackthorn clan, cut off by the Synthet invasion</td></tr><tr><td>Forbidden Grove</td><td>Sacred grove that only initiates may enter</td></tr><tr><td>Spring of Life</td><td>The Vitals' holiest relic, on a lake island in the Forbidden Grove; Diamanda takes the Vital ether here</td></tr><tr><td>Golden Ash-Tree City</td><td>The Vital capital, home of the dragon Musician and an ancient treant</td></tr></table><h2>Lands of the Chaots</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Land of Chaos</td><td>The Chaot country</td></tr><tr><td>Fire Valley</td><td>Konnar's home</td></tr><tr><td>Chaos Eye</td><td>The holiest Chaot altar, in a volcano crater, guarded by the Great Shaman; Diamanda takes the ether of Chaos here</td></tr><tr><td>City of Thundering Stones</td><td>Chaot city taken by a Kinet night raid</td></tr><tr><td>Western and Eastern Castles</td><td>Seats of the two halves of the race</td></tr><tr><td>Main Arena</td><td>Where the West and the East settle disputes with duels of monsters</td></tr><tr><td>Island Fortress</td><td>Fortress fought over by Chaots and Kinets</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Island</td><td>The Kinets' price for peace</td></tr></table><h2>Lands of the Kinets</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Lands of Movement</td><td>The Kinet country</td></tr><tr><td>City of Eight Gates</td><td>The thousand-year-old Kinet capital, overrun by the Pale Plague</td></tr><tr><td>Market Place</td><td>Trading district of the capital</td></tr><tr><td>Fish Island</td><td>Where the Head of the Sixteen was caged</td></tr><tr><td>Temple of Order</td><td>Its priests find the rite that weakens the plague</td></tr><tr><td>Shrine of Order</td><td>Kinet holy place that shields their lands from hostile magic</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Peninsula</td><td>On the border with the Synthets; the White Lord's portal opens here</td></tr></table><h2>Lands of the Synthets</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Land of Synthesis</td><td>The Synthet country</td></tr><tr><td>Axis City</td><td>The Synthet capital, consumed by the Pale Rust</td></tr><tr><td>Shrine of Synthesis</td><td>Synthet holy place that shields their lands from hostile magic</td></tr><tr><td>Abandoned City</td><td>Where the White Lord's whirlwind devoured the souls of mighty heroes and opened the portal</td></tr></table><p>See also the pages of the four races: <a href=\"wiki://chaots\">Chaots</a>, <a href=\"wiki://kinets\">Kinets</a>, <a href=\"wiki://vitals\">Vitals</a> and <a href=\"wiki://synthets\">Synthets</a>.</p>", "search": "places the named places of the world of ether, as the briefings, dialogues and map labels give them. the plots are on the story of etherlords and the story of etherlords ii. the temple of time the temple of time stands at the edge of the world. it is the seat of the white lord and the goal of every lord in a time of changes; the first to enter it is said to become the new white lord. in the first game the road to it opens only once a rival race is beaten, and only a lord may take the last step inside. in etherlords ii the white lord's castle and his portal to the outer plane stand on the northern peninsula. see the white lord. places of etherlords lost city chebokkiana, the ancient capital of the vitals, buried by a forbidden ritual when the synthets attacked it. in the ruins of its town centre lies the key to the temple of time foggy forest home of an ancient tomb guarded by ghostly keepers; its fog brings illusions and weak sight wild wasteland lair of the fire konung, whose strength comes from four altars of war vault of true knowledge kinet archive with documents from the beginning of time sacred forest vital forest, which has grown and died eight times since the lost city fell red mountains chaot homeland, which gives its name to the chaots' law wooden cage the prison of trees where a legendary hero waits to be freed snowflake maze ice labyrinth of the yearly kinet tournament borderlands where the lands of all four races meet; the road to new lands is said to begin there lands of the vitals forest land the vital country blackthorn fortress northern fortress of the blackthorn clan, cut off by the synthet invasion forbidden grove sacred grove that only initiates may enter spring of life the vitals' holiest relic, on a lake island in the forbidden grove; diamanda takes the vital ether here golden ash-tree city the vital capital, home of the dragon musician and an ancient treant lands of the chaots land of chaos the chaot country fire valley konnar's home chaos eye the holiest chaot altar, in a volcano crater, guarded by the great shaman; diamanda takes the ether of chaos here city of thundering stones chaot city taken by a kinet night raid western and eastern castles seats of the two halves of the race main arena where the west and the east settle disputes with duels of monsters island fortress fortress fought over by chaots and kinets northern island the kinets' price for peace lands of the kinets lands of movement the kinet country city of eight gates the thousand-year-old kinet capital, overrun by the pale plague market place trading district of the capital fish island where the head of the sixteen was caged temple of order its priests find the rite that weakens the plague shrine of order kinet holy place that shields their lands from hostile magic northern peninsula on the border with the synthets; the white lord's portal opens here lands of the synthets land of synthesis the synthet country axis city the synthet capital, consumed by the pale rust shrine of synthesis synthet holy place that shields their lands from hostile magic abandoned city where the white lord's whirlwind devoured the souls of mighty heroes and opened the portal see also the pages of the four races: chaots, kinets, vitals and synthets."},
		{"key": "story-el1", "title": "The story of Etherlords", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>The story of Etherlords</h1><p class=\"note\">This page describes the whole plot of both Etherlords campaigns, endings included.</p><p>The story of Etherlords is told in the briefings read before each campaign chapter. The background is on <a href=\"wiki://story-world\">The world of ether</a> and the races on <a href=\"wiki://story-peoples\">Races and peoples</a>.</p><p>Names follow the game's English texts. The few that have no English text (two chapter titles and some terms) are translated from the Russian edition and marked <em>(tr.)</em>.</p><h2>The Time of Changes</h2><p>Once in thousands of years the heavenly bodies line up with the centre of the universe and white ether floods the world. Old barriers fail, mountain passes open and the road to the <strong>Temple of Time</strong> at the edge of the world becomes passable. Legend says that the first Lord to enter the Temple becomes the new <strong>White Lord</strong>, master of white ether, and the old one is banished.</p><p>The long peace ends. Each Lord seeks an ally for the race to the Temple:</p><ul><li>the Lord of Synthesis offers his hand to the Lord of Chaos: strength going hand in hand with perfection, the alliance of <strong>Iron and Fury</strong> <em>(tr.)</em>;</li><li>the Lord of Vitality, who hates war and death, joins the Lord of Motion, the lord of harmony and order: the alliance of <strong>Life and Wisdom</strong> <em>(tr.)</em>.</li></ul><p>These two alliances are the two <a href=\"wiki://campaign-el1\">campaigns</a>. Each has eight chapters; within a campaign the chapters alternate between the two allied races.</p><h2>Flaming Iron: Chaots and Synthets</h2><ol><li><strong>Fire Ordeal</strong>. The Chaots must prove that their warrior spirit has not faded in the long peace. A Fire Spirit has appeared near their castle for the first time in centuries, and legend says only the hero held in the Wooden Cage can defeat it.</li><li><strong>Fire and Ice</strong>. With the passes open, the old feud with the Kinets flares up. The Chaots destroy the Kinet castle so it cannot strike them from behind while their best warriors march to the Temple.</li><li><strong>Unexpected Help</strong>. The Synthets take over: Vitals and Kinets counter-attack a Chaot border castle, and the Synthets come to defend their allies, whom they privately value as experimental material.</li><li><strong>Capture</strong>. The Synthets ask a favour: their rituals need one of the strongest Kinets. The Chaots hunt down <strong>Diamanda the Magnificent, Mistress of Dragons</strong>, and must wear her down without killing her.</li><li><strong>Fire Konung</strong>. Next on the Synthets' path to perfection is the Fire Konung, a mysterious being of flesh near the Wild Wasteland whose strength is bound to four Altars of War.</li><li><strong>Lost City</strong>. The way to the Lost City, the ancient capital of the Vitals, is open. A legendary artifact, the <strong>Key</strong> to the Temple, survived there among horrors born of an ancient forbidden ritual. Beside the ruins the Chaots meet a monster, the Walking Horror.</li><li><strong>Temple of Time</strong>. The alliance breaks, and the player chooses a side: - <em>Chaots</em>: the monster in the Lost City was a Synthet creature, surely part of a treacherous plan. The Synthets refuse to explain, and the Chaots punish them under the Law of Red Mountains. - <em>Synthets</em>: the alliance has become a burden and the Chaot castle happens to stand on the road. The Synthets' Chief Oracle, who suspects the White Lord is only a fluctuation of information, sends heroes to check.</li><li><strong>Last Battle</strong>. The chosen race's envoy reaches the Temple gates. Only a Lord may take the final step, so the Lord fights the White Lord in person, twice, to prove himself worthy of the throne.</li></ol><h2>Flying Leaf: Vitals and Kinets</h2><ol><li><strong>Mechanical Threat</strong> <em>(tr.)</em>. The Vitals' Book of Life speaks of the Temple and of a key that turns the Wheel of Time. A hideous Synthet machine now roams the Vital forests, and only the hero in the Wooden Cage can defeat it.</li><li><strong>New Lands</strong> <em>(tr.)</em>. The Chaots attack, fearsome in battle but, the Vitals say, weak of mind and overconfident. The Vitals destroy their forward castle.</li><li><strong>Help to Vitals</strong>. The Kinets see the Chaot and Synthet alliance as a deadly danger to Stability and Order and come to their ally's aid.</li><li><strong>Aviak's Nest</strong>. The Synthets have lured a flock of Aviaks, once subject to the Kinets, into their lands. The birds turn mad and disobedient, and the Vitals destroy the flock before the Synthets can use it.</li><li><strong>Ancient Tomb</strong>. Kinet records place the Key in the Lost City, and the way there can only be learned at an ancient tomb in the Foggy Forest, guarded by ghostly Keepers.</li><li><strong>Lost City</strong>. The Vitals enter their ancient capital, Chebokiana, to fetch the Key and hand it to the Kinets. Beside the Key stands a Synthet hero named <strong>Diamanda</strong>.</li><li><strong>Temple of Time</strong>. The alliance breaks over Diamanda's fate: - <em>Vitals</em>: the Kinets, for the sake of Stability, handed their own heroine to the Synthets. The Vitals keep the Key and go to the Temple alone. - <em>Kinets</em>: the Vitals took the Key at the price of Diamanda. The Kinets believe the Vitals killed her without trying to save her, while the Vitals accuse the Kinets of plotting with the Synthets. The Kinets set out to establish a new order.</li><li><strong>Last Battle</strong>. As in the other campaign, the Lord duels the White Lord at the Temple.</li></ol><p>Each side blames the other for Diamanda's fate, but the Flaming Iron campaign shows that the Chaots captured her for the Synthets. The first game never says outright what the Synthets did with her: the Lost City map simply puts a Synthet hero, \"Omega-800D Diamanda\", where she stands. Etherlords II confirms it: Synthet scientists turned the Kinet heroine into a winged Synthet warrior.</p><h2>Endings</h2><p>Each of the four races has its own closing film, shown after the White Lord is defeated. Following the legend, the victorious Lord takes the White Lord's place as ruler of all ether. Diamanda's story continues in <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">Etherlords II</a>.</p><h2>Other missions</h2><p>The single missions are separate skirmishes with short premises: a land of crystal lakes, the legacy of an ancient Summoner, an unusual alliance of Vitals and Chaots against the Kinets, and so on. The multiplayer maps set two to four races against each other on borderlands and ore fields.</p>", "search": "the story of etherlords this page describes the whole plot of both etherlords campaigns, endings included. the story of etherlords is told in the briefings read before each campaign chapter. the background is on the world of ether and the races on races and peoples. names follow the game's english texts. the few that have no english text (two chapter titles and some terms) are translated from the russian edition and marked (tr.). the time of changes once in thousands of years the heavenly bodies line up with the centre of the universe and white ether floods the world. old barriers fail, mountain passes open and the road to the temple of time at the edge of the world becomes passable. legend says that the first lord to enter the temple becomes the new white lord, master of white ether, and the old one is banished. the long peace ends. each lord seeks an ally for the race to the temple: the lord of synthesis offers his hand to the lord of chaos: strength going hand in hand with perfection, the alliance of iron and fury (tr.); the lord of vitality, who hates war and death, joins the lord of motion, the lord of harmony and order: the alliance of life and wisdom (tr.). these two alliances