the white lord in person, twice, to prove himself worthy of the throne. flying leaf: vitals and kinets mechanical threat (tr.). the vitals' book of life speaks of the temple and of a key that turns the wheel of time. a hideous synthet machine now roams the vital forests, and only the hero in the wooden cage can defeat it. new lands (tr.). the chaots attack, fearsome in battle but, the vitals say, weak of mind and overconfident. the vitals destroy their forward castle. help to vitals. the kinets see the chaot and synthet alliance as a deadly danger to stability and order and come to their ally's aid. aviak's nest. the synthets have lured a flock of aviaks, once subject to the kinets, into their lands. the birds turn mad and disobedient, and the vitals destroy the flock before the synthets can use it. ancient tomb. kinet records place the key in the lost city, and the way there can only be learned at an ancient tomb in the foggy forest, guarded by ghostly keepers. lost city. the vitals enter their ancient capital, chebokiana, to fetch the key and hand it to the kinets. beside the key stands a synthet hero named diamanda. temple of time. the alliance breaks over diamanda's fate: - vitals: the kinets, for the sake of stability, handed their own heroine to the synthets. the vitals keep the key and go to the temple alone. - kinets: the vitals took the key at the price of diamanda. the kinets believe the vitals killed her without trying to save her, while the vitals accuse the kinets of plotting with the synthets. the kinets set out to establish a new order. last battle. as in the other campaign, the lord duels the white lord at the temple. each side blames the other for diamanda's fate, but the flaming iron campaign shows that the chaots captured her for the synthets. the first game never says outright what the synthets did with her: the lost city map simply puts a synthet hero, \"omega-800d diamanda\", where she stands. etherlords ii confirms it: synthet scientists turned the kinet heroine into a winged synthet warrior. endings each of the four races has its own closing film, shown after the white lord is defeated. following the legend, the victorious lord takes the white lord's place as ruler of all ether. diamanda's story continues in etherlords ii. other missions the single missions are separate skirmishes with short premises: a land of crystal lakes, the legacy of an ancient summoner, an unusual alliance of vitals and chaots against the kinets, and so on. the multiplayer maps set two to four races against each other on borderlands and ore fields."},
		{"key": "story-el2", "title": "The story of Etherlords II", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>The story of Etherlords II</h1><p class=\"note\">This page describes the whole plot of Etherlords II, endings included.</p><p>Etherlords II tells one story five times. The four race campaigns start at the same moment and show the same events through different eyes, often on the same maps; the last campaign, Diamanda's, reveals what was behind them. The <a href=\"wiki://campaign-el2\">campaign order</a> is: Vitals or Chaots first, then Synthets or Kinets, then Diamanda. The background is on <a href=\"wiki://story-world\">The world of ether</a>, the people on <a href=\"wiki://story-characters\">Characters</a>.</p><h2>Before the game</h2><p>A long peace has passed since the events of <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">Etherlords</a>, and a new Time of Change is near. The <strong>White Lord</strong> fears that it will bring a new Lord to take his throne, as it has before. To end the cycle for good he means to drain the flows of ether and turn the races into docile pale shadows.</p><p>He sensed another world, the <strong>Outer Plane</strong>, behind an ancient sealed portal. With ghostly visions he lured mighty heroes of all four races to the <strong>Abandoned City</strong>, where his whirlwind devoured their souls; the shock opened a <strong>Portal</strong> on the bleak northern peninsula, and only his will keeps it open. Through it come the <strong>Pale</strong> creatures, which suck ether and colour out of the world.</p><p>At the edge of the world, among the rising souls of dead heroes, he found the soul of <strong>Diamanda</strong>, the Kinet heroine whom the Synthets once turned into a warrior of their own and whom the Vital heroine Ciara destroyed. Its black and blue currents were still fighting each other. He healed her body, found in a northern crypt, and woke her as his puppet: she is to absorb all four ethers for a ritual that will keep the Portal open forever.</p><h2>Vitals: Eileen of Rowan clan</h2><p>The young heroine <strong>Eileen</strong> is sent north by the Vital elders.</p><ol><li><strong>Synthet Incursion.</strong> There has been no news from the Blackthorn Fortress. Its commander Tienan reports a Synthet invasion: on its first day the ether flows twisted, the teleporters went mad and Synthets began to come out of them. Eileen relieves the outpost that Kilan the Fearless held alone for eight days. The hermit Rulana has a vision of an armoured woman, \"like a Synthet but with clear eyes\", walking south.</li><li><strong>The Spring of Life.</strong> Synthets enter the Forbidden Grove, where only initiates may go. Eileen enters anyway and meets a strange \"scout\" who completes a ritual at the Spring and escapes.</li><li><strong>Golden Ash-Tree City.</strong> The Vital capital is besieged by all three other races, and the first pale creatures appear: hollow shells that do not smell of machines. Eileen frees the city's dragon Musician, the ancient Treant who keeps the walls standing, and Elder Vian's son Narmes.</li><li><strong>Traitor!</strong> In a Synthet-held frontier fortress Eileen defeats Flon, a Vital who sold himself to the Synthets, and his Walking Horror. Vian compares her to the great Ciara, who once destroyed the terrible Diamanda, and Eileen realises that the \"scout\" at the Spring matched Diamanda's description.</li></ol><p>The Vital campaign ends with the mystery unsolved.</p><h2>Chaots: Konnar of Fire Valley</h2><p><strong>Konnar</strong> is a young warrior after loot and glory.</p><ol><li><strong>The Kinet Threat.</strong> The Kinets, who never invade, have crossed a lake on bridges of ice and killed the chief Hellvard. With a hammer hardened in the Flame of Chaos, Konnar breaks the bridges.</li><li><strong>The Chaos Eye.</strong> At the holy altar in a volcano crater pilgrims are vanishing. Konnar glimpses a half-Synthet, half-Kinet warrior from the old tales, but arrives too late: the Great Shaman is already dead.</li><li><strong>City of Thundering Stones.</strong> The city fell to a night raid while the West and the East quarrelled. Alveig, Lightning of the West, and Krogerd, Hammer of the East, settle who leads the war with a duel of monsters: her Fighting Dragon against his Cyclops. Konnar frees the dragon and the city.</li><li><strong>The Island Fortress.</strong> Konnar breaks through to the Kinet peninsula. The Kinet leaders ask for peace, saying a great misfortune drove them to war. Alveig accepts and takes the Northern Island as the price.</li></ol><h2>Kinets: Mirabell</h2><p><strong>Mirabell</strong> is a young, very formal messenger with a love of calligraphy.</p><ol><li><strong>More than a Messenger.</strong> Sent to invite the noble Rinalda to the Council of Sixteen, which has met over the <strong>Pale Plague</strong>, Mirabell finds her trapped on an island and slowly drained by the plague. A warrior much like Diamanda broke the bridge behind her. Mirabell rebuilds it and frees Rinalda in time.</li><li><strong>A Fortress Siege.</strong> The plague is eating Kinet lands, and the Council orders the Big Campaign to take new land from the Chaots: the same siege of the Island Fortress that Konnar fights from the other side.</li><li><strong>The Council of Sixteen.</strong> The plague reaches the capital. Mirabell finds that the Keeper of Keys lured the Head of the Sixteen into a cage, claiming that the White Lord demanded his blood, to take his seat. Priests of the Temple of Order teach her a rite that unites the souls of all four races and a pale monster, and it weakens the plague.</li><li><strong>Confrontation.</strong> The seer Droncan foretells that whoever defeats Diamanda on the northern peninsula will end the plague. There the Synthet hero Alpha admits Diamanda is too strong for him, and Mirabell defeats her.</li></ol><h2>Synthets: Alpha-100F \"Steel\"</h2><p><strong>Alpha</strong> is a disciplined Synthet soldier.</p><ol><li><strong>Discovery.</strong> Alpha frees the hero Beta from a dungeon where the <strong>Pale Rust</strong> is draining him. Beta recognises his captor from deep memory: Diamanda, \"daughter of Air\", long dead.</li><li><strong>Assault on the Fortress.</strong> With their northern lands lost to the Rust, the Synthets seek a foothold in the south. The Vital traitor Flon shows them a secret passage, and Alpha turns a teleporter to bring Walking Horrors into a Vital fortress.</li><li><strong>Save Axis City.</strong> The Rust swallows the Synthet capital. The records of a scientist who turned himself into a measuring device and went mad reveal that a powerful pale monster is a funnel through which ether leaves the world. Alpha destroys it, and the Rust recedes from the city.</li><li><strong>Trail's End.</strong> The strongest funnel is on the northern peninsula, where Diamanda waits. Mirabell, beaten by her, leaves the fight to Alpha, who defeats Diamanda. The scientists find only a fragment of a teleporter.</li></ol><p>The Kinet and Synthet finales are the same battle from two sides, and in each the player's hero strikes the last blow. Diamanda's campaign suggests that neither race really ended the plague.</p><h2>Diamanda</h2><p>The last campaign, called the story of the White Lord, opens with his monologue (see <em>Before the game</em>).</p><ol><li><strong>Awakening.</strong> To wake her soul, Diamanda must destroy a former \"brother\". She chooses a teleporter: to the Kinet lands, where she traps Rinalda on her island, or to the Synthet lands, where she traps Beta. If she is too slow, Mirabell or Alpha frees the prisoner and the White Lord abandons her. Afterwards fragments of memory return: bright clothes of heroes, a blinding flash, a long sleep, and a question of whose will drives her.</li><li><strong>Theft of Vitality.</strong> She kills the Keeper of the Spring of Life and takes the Vital ether, while the White Lord deals with Eileen. Marvelling at the living forest, she asks her master whether he really wants to destroy it.</li><li><strong>Violation of Chaos.</strong> At the Chaos Eye she kills the Great Shaman and takes the ether of Chaos, escaping Konnar. (Chapters 2 and 3 come in either order, depending on her first choice.)</li><li><strong>The Final Awakening.</strong> Back at the Portal, Diamanda understands that her master lied about bringing calm: he is mad with the desire for immortality. He tells her that, having merged all four ethers, she has become the greatest artifact in the universe, and her sacrifice will keep the Portal open forever. She defeats his mighty guardian and kills the White Lord.</li></ol><h2>Ending</h2><p>With the White Lord dead, his spells break. The heroes of the world of ether will cleanse their lands and believe they defeated the pale disease themselves. Diamanda decides that the Portal must vanish without a trace, and she stays on guard until it closes, sensing that her destiny is about to be fulfilled.</p><p>The game does not say whether the \"Diamanda\" defeated by Mirabell or Alpha on the northern peninsula, of whom only a fragment of a teleporter remained, was the guardian of the closing Portal.</p>", "search": "the story of etherlords ii this page describes the whole plot of etherlords ii, endings included. etherlords ii tells one story five times. the four race campaigns start at the same moment and show the same events through different eyes, often on the same maps; the last campaign, diamanda's, reveals what was behind them. the campaign order is: vitals or chaots first, then synthets or kinets, then diamanda. the background is on the world of ether, the people on characters. before the game a long peace has passed since the events of etherlords, and a new time of change is near. the white lord fears that it will bring a new lord to take his throne, as it has before. to end the cycle for good he means to drain the flows of ether and turn the races into docile pale shadows. he sensed another world, the outer plane, behind an ancient sealed portal. with ghostly visions he lured mighty heroes of all four races to the abandoned city, where his whirlwind devoured their souls; the shock opened a portal on the bleak northern peninsula, and only his will keeps it open. through it come the pale creatures, which suck ether and colour out of the world. at the edge of the world, among the rising souls of dead heroes, he found the soul of diamanda, the kinet heroine whom the synthets once turned into a warrior of their own and whom the vital heroine ciara destroyed. its black and blue currents were still fighting each other. he healed her body, found in a northern crypt, and woke her as his puppet: she is to absorb all four ethers for a ritual that will keep the portal open forever. vitals: eileen of rowan clan the young heroine eileen is sent north by the vital elders. synthet incursion. there has been no news from the blackthorn fortress. its commander tienan reports a synthet invasion: on its first day the ether flows twisted, the teleporters went mad and synthets began to come out of them. eileen relieves the outpost that kilan the fearless held alone for eight days. the hermit rulana has a vision of an armoured woman, \"like a synthet but with clear eyes\", walking south. the spring of life. synthets enter the forbidden grove, where only initiates may go. eileen enters anyway and meets a strange \"scout\" who completes a ritual at the spring and escapes. golden ash-tree city. the vital capital is besieged by all three other races, and the first pale creatures appear: hollow shells that do not smell of machines. eileen frees the city's dragon musician, the ancient treant who keeps the walls standing, and elder vian's son narmes. traitor! in a synthet-held frontier fortress eileen defeats flon, a vital who sold himself to the synthets, and his walking horror. vian compares her to the great ciara, who once destroyed the terrible diamanda, and eileen realises that the \"scout\" at the spring matched diamanda's description. the vital campaign ends with the mystery unsolved. chaots: konnar of fire valley konnar is a young warrior after loot and glory. the kinet threat. the kinets, who never invade, have crossed a lake on bridges of ice and killed the chief hellvard. with a hammer hardened in the flame of chaos, konnar breaks the bridges. the chaos eye. at the holy altar in a volcano crater pilgrims are vanishing. konnar glimpses a half-synthet, half-kinet warrior from the old tales, but arrives too late: the great shaman is already dead. city of thundering stones. the city fell to a night raid while the west and the east quarrelled. alveig, lightning of the west, and krogerd, hammer of the east, settle who leads the war with a duel of monsters: her fighting dragon against his cyclops. konnar frees the dragon and the city. the island fortress. konnar breaks through to the kinet peninsula. the kinet leaders ask for peace, saying a great misfortune drove them to war. alveig accepts and takes the northern island as the price. kinets: mirabell mirabell is a young, very formal messenger with a love of calligraphy. more than a messenger. sent to invite the noble rinalda to the council of sixteen, which has met over the pale plague, mirabell finds her trapped on an island and slowly drained by the plague. a warrior much like diamanda broke the bridge behind her. mirabell rebuilds it and frees rinalda in time. a fortress siege. the plague is eating kinet lands, and the council orders the big campaign to take new land from the chaots: the same siege of the island fortress that konnar fights from the other side. the council of sixteen. the plague reaches the capital. mirabell finds that the keeper of keys lured the head of the sixteen into a cage, claiming that the white lord demanded his blood, to take his seat. priests of the temple of order teach her a rite that unites the souls of all four races and a pale monster, and it weakens the plague. confrontation. the seer droncan foretells that whoever defeats diamanda on the northern peninsula will end the plague. there the synthet hero alpha admits diamanda is too strong for him, and mirabell defeats her. synthets: alpha-100f \"steel\" alpha is a disciplined synthet soldier. discovery. alpha frees the hero beta from a dungeon where the pale rust is draining him. beta recognises his captor from deep memory: diamanda, \"daughter of air\", long dead. assault on the fortress. with their northern lands lost to the rust, the synthets seek a foothold in the south. the vital traitor flon shows them a secret passage, and alpha turns a teleporter to bring walking horrors into a vital fortress. save axis city. the rust swallows the synthet capital. the records of a scientist who turned himself into a measuring device and went mad reveal that a powerful pale monster is a funnel through which ether leaves the world. alpha destroys it, and the rust recedes from the city. trail's end. the strongest funnel is on the northern peninsula, where diamanda waits. mirabell, beaten by her, leaves the fight to alpha, who defeats diamanda. the scientists find only a fragment of a teleporter. the kinet and synthet finales are the same battle from two sides, and in each the player's hero strikes the last blow. diamanda's campaign suggests that neither race really ended the plague. diamanda the last campaign, called the story of the white lord, opens with his monologue (see before the game). awakening. to wake her soul, diamanda must destroy a former \"brother\". she chooses a teleporter: to the kinet lands, where she traps rinalda on her island, or to the synthet lands, where she traps beta. if she is too slow, mirabell or alpha frees the prisoner and the white lord abandons her. afterwards fragments of memory return: bright clothes of heroes, a blinding flash, a long sleep, and a question of whose will drives her. theft of vitality. she kills the keeper of the spring of life and takes the vital ether, while the white lord deals with eileen. marvelling at the living forest, she asks her master whether he really wants to destroy it. violation of chaos. at the chaos eye she kills the great shaman and takes the ether of chaos, escaping konnar. (chapters 2 and 3 come in either order, depending on her first choice.) the final awakening. back at the portal, diamanda understands that her master lied about bringing calm: he is mad with the desire for immortality. he tells her that, having merged all four ethers, she has become the greatest artifact in the universe, and her sacrifice will keep the portal open forever. she defeats his mighty guardian and kills the white lord. ending with the white lord dead, his spells break. the heroes of the world of ether will cleanse their lands and believe they defeated the pale disease themselves. diamanda decides that the portal must vanish without a trace, and she stays on guard until it closes, sensing that her destiny is about to be fulfilled. the game does not say whether the \"diamanda\" defeated by mirabell or alpha on the northern peninsula, of whom only a fragment of a teleporter remained, was the guardian of the closing portal."},
		{"key": "diamanda", "title": "Diamanda", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Diamanda</h1><p class=\"note\">This page reveals the whole of Diamanda's story.</p><p><strong>Diamanda</strong> is the one character who runs through both games. She begins as a Kinet heroine, becomes a Synthet warrior, dies, returns as the White Lord's puppet and ends as his killer. Etherlords II calls her \"the ancient creation of heroes' magic\".</p><h2>In Etherlords</h2><p>In the first game she is <strong>Diamanda the Magnificent, Lady of Dragons</strong>, a Kinet heroine of great power: every dragon of the world serves the Kinets. During the Time of Changes she ventures into Synthet lands, where the Chaots, at their Synthet allies' request, capture her alive for the Synthets' rituals.</p><p>The Synthet scientists turn her into a Synthet warrior, something possible only in a Time of Change. In the Lost City the Vitals find her standing beside the Key to the Temple as the Synthet hero \"Omega-800D Diamanda\". Her loss breaks the alliance of Vitals and Kinets: each blames the other, and neither knows the truth. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</p><h2>The terrible Diamanda</h2><p>Etherlords II remembers her as a legend. Synthet and Kinet natures were joined in her; she wore armour and had huge wings, and she terrorised the Vitals until the Vital heroine <strong>Ciara</strong> destroyed her. The Synthet hero Beta still recognises her from deep memory as \"daughter of Air\".</p><h2>The White Lord's puppet</h2><p>At the edge of the world, where the souls of dead heroes rise, the White Lord found her soul: two currents of ether, black and blue, still fighting each other. He healed her body, found in a northern crypt, and woke her without a will of her own. Because she already held two ethers, he ordered her to master the remaining two.</p><p>In the four race campaigns of Etherlords II she is the mysterious armoured stranger: she traps a Kinet or Synthet hero, kills the Keeper of the Spring of Life and the Great Shaman of the Chaots, and waits on the northern peninsula, where Mirabell or Alpha defeats \"Diamanda\". See <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p><h2>Awakening</h2><p>Her own campaign tells the other side. With each deed her memories return: the bright clothes of heroes, a blinding flash, a long sleep. She marvels at the life of the Vital forest and the fire of the Chaots and asks her master whether he really means to destroy them.</p><p>Back at the Portal she understands that he lied: he is mad with the desire for immortality. Having merged all four ethers, she has become, in his words, the greatest artifact in the universe, and her sacrifice is to keep the Portal open forever. She defeats his guardian, kills the White Lord, and stays to guard the Portal until it closes, letting the heroes of the world believe they won alone.</p><h2>In play</h2><p>In Etherlords she is a level 8 Kinet hero with the specialization of casting spells for free; as Omega-800D she is a level 12 Synthet. In Etherlords II she is the hero of the training camp and of the last campaign, and her portrait changes as she absorbs the ethers of Life and Chaos. See also <a href=\"wiki://white-lord\">The White Lord</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-characters\">Characters</a>.</p>", "search": "diamanda this page reveals the whole of diamanda's story. diamanda is the one character who runs through both games. she begins as a kinet heroine, becomes a synthet warrior, dies, returns as the white lord's puppet and ends as his killer. etherlords ii calls her \"the ancient creation of heroes' magic\". in etherlords in the first game she is diamanda the magnificent, lady of dragons, a kinet heroine of great power: every dragon of the world serves the kinets. during the time of changes she ventures into synthet lands, where the chaots, at their synthet allies' request, capture her alive for the synthets' rituals. the synthet scientists turn her into a synthet warrior, something possible only in a time of change. in the lost city the vitals find her standing beside the key to the temple as the synthet hero \"omega-800d diamanda\". her loss breaks the alliance of vitals and kinets: each blames the other, and neither knows the truth. see the story of etherlords. the terrible diamanda etherlords ii remembers her as a legend. synthet and kinet natures were joined in her; she wore armour and had huge wings, and she terrorised the vitals until the vital heroine ciara destroyed her. the synthet hero beta still recognises her from deep memory as \"daughter of air\". the white lord's puppet at the edge of the world, where the souls of dead heroes rise, the white lord found her soul: two currents of ether, black and blue, still fighting each other. he healed her body, found in a northern crypt, and woke her without a will of her own. because she already held two ethers, he ordered her to master the remaining two. in the four race campaigns of etherlords ii she is the mysterious armoured stranger: she traps a kinet or synthet hero, kills the keeper of the spring of life and the great shaman of the chaots, and waits on the northern peninsula, where mirabell or alpha defeats \"diamanda\". see the story of etherlords ii. awakening her own campaign tells the other side. with each deed her memories return: the bright clothes of heroes, a blinding flash, a long sleep. she marvels at the life of the vital forest and the fire of the chaots and asks her master whether he really means to destroy them. back at the portal she understands that he lied: he is mad with the desire for immortality. having merged all four ethers, she has become, in his words, the greatest artifact in the universe, and her sacrifice is to keep the portal open forever. she defeats his guardian, kills the white lord, and stays to guard the portal until it closes, letting the heroes of the world believe they won alone. in play in etherlords she is a level 8 kinet hero with the specialization of casting spells for free; as omega-800d she is a level 12 synthet. in etherlords ii she is the hero of the training camp and of the last campaign, and her portrait changes as she absorbs the ethers of life and chaos. see also the white lord and characters.", "heroes": [["el1", "Diamanda", "Kinet heroine (Etherlords)"], ["el1", "Omega-800D \"Diamanda\"", "Omega-800D, the Synthet (Etherlords)"], ["el2", "Diamanda1Blue", "Awakened (Etherlords II)"], ["el2", "Diamanda4Green", "Last form (Etherlords II)"]]},
		{"key": "white-lord", "title": "The White Lord", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>The White Lord</h1><p class=\"note\">This page reveals the White Lord's fate.</p><p>The <strong>White Lord</strong> is the master of white ether, the source of all other ethers, and the ruler of the world of ether. He lives in the Temple of Time at the edge of the world. The first game calls him the Lord of all Lords and the master of all living creatures in the world of ether; Etherlords II, the keeper of balance. See <a href=\"wiki://story-world\">The world of ether</a>.</p><h2>The legend</h2><p>Legend says the first Lord to enter the Temple of Time becomes the new White Lord, and the old one is banished to a distant, hostile world. This is why every Time of Changes, when the road to the Temple opens, turns the races against each other.</p><p>Not everyone believes in him. In the first game the Synthets' Chief Oracle concluded that the White Lord was most likely an erroneous fluctuation of information energy, or a hallucination, and sent heroes to check.</p><h2>In Etherlords</h2><p>At the end of each campaign the Lord of the victorious race reaches the Temple gates. Only a Lord may take the last step, so the Lord fights the White Lord in person, in two battles, to prove worthy of the throne. In the game he is a hero of level 15 and then 20, with the specialization Dragon Skin. See <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</p><h2>In Etherlords II</h2><p>Countless days have passed since he took his throne, and he fears the next Time of Change and the usurper it will bring. To end the cycle he means to drain the ether flows and make the races docile pale shadows, calling it bringing calm and establishing order.</p><p>He lured mighty heroes to the Abandoned City and devoured their souls to open a <strong>Portal</strong> to the Outer Plane, letting in the <a href=\"wiki://pale\">Pale</a>. He raised <strong>Diamanda</strong> from the dead as his puppet and sent her to steal the ethers of the races, giving her fifty days and threatening to abandon her as a soulless shadow if she failed. His true aim was to sacrifice her, once she held all four ethers, to keep the Portal open forever.</p><p>In the Kinet capital a plotter used his name as cover, claiming the White Lord demanded the blood of the Head of the Sixteen. A Kinet black dragon in Synthet lands spoke of a coming Master heard in the music of the Spheres.</p><h2>His end</h2><p>Diamanda saw through him. At his castle by the Portal she offered him a warrior's choice: give up his reign, or leave for the pale world and let the Portal close behind him. He answered that it did not matter whether she came obedient or rebellious. She defeated his guardian, a colourless <a href=\"wiki://card-ekon\">Konung</a>, and killed him. His enchantments broke, and the Portal began to close. See <a href=\"wiki://diamanda\">Diamanda</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p>", "search": "the white lord this page reveals the white lord's fate. the white lord is the master of white ether, the source of all other ethers, and the ruler of the world of ether. he lives in the temple of time at the edge of the world. the first game calls him the lord of all lords and the master of all living creatures in the world of ether; etherlords ii, the keeper of balance. see the world of ether. the legend legend says the first lord to enter the temple of time becomes the new white lord, and the old one is banished to a distant, hostile world. this is why every time of changes, when the road to the temple opens, turns the races against each other. not everyone believes in him. in the first game the synthets' chief oracle concluded that the white lord was most likely an erroneous fluctuation of information energy, or a hallucination, and sent heroes to check. in etherlords at the end of each campaign the lord of the victorious race reaches the temple gates. only a lord may take the last step, so the lord fights the white lord in person, in two battles, to prove worthy of the throne. in the game he is a hero of level 15 and then 20, with the specialization dragon skin. see the story of etherlords. in etherlords ii countless days have passed since he took his throne, and he fears the next time of change and the usurper it will bring. to end the cycle he means to drain the ether flows and make the races docile pale shadows, calling it bringing calm and establishing order. he lured mighty heroes to the abandoned city and devoured their souls to open a portal to the outer plane, letting in the pale. he raised diamanda from the dead as his puppet and sent her to steal the ethers of the races, giving her fifty days and threatening to abandon her as a soulless shadow if she failed. his true aim was to sacrifice her, once she held all four ethers, to keep the portal open forever. in the kinet capital a plotter used his name as cover, claiming the white lord demanded the blood of the head of the sixteen. a kinet black dragon in synthet lands spoke of a coming master heard in the music of the spheres. his end diamanda saw through him. at his castle by the portal she offered him a warrior's choice: give up his reign, or leave for the pale world and let the portal close behind him. he answered that it did not matter whether she came obedient or rebellious. she defeated his guardian, a colourless konung, and killed him. his enchantments broke, and the portal began to close. see diamanda and the story of etherlords ii.", "heroes": [["el1", "White Lord I", "The White Lord (Etherlords)"]]},
		{"key": "story-characters", "title": "Characters", "category": "story", "html": "<h1>Characters</h1><p class=\"note\">This page reveals the fates of the main characters.</p><p>The named characters of both games. The two central ones have pages of their own: <a href=\"wiki://diamanda\">Diamanda</a> and <a href=\"wiki://white-lord\">The White Lord</a>. The plots are on <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a> and <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p><h2>The White Lord and Diamanda</h2><ul><li><strong>The White Lord.</strong> Master of white ether, who lives in the Temple of Time. In the first game the Lord of the victorious race duels him for his throne. In Etherlords II, to escape the cycle that brings a new usurper every Time of Change, he opens a portal to the Outer Plane, raises Diamanda as his tool and plans to sacrifice her. She kills him.</li><li><strong>Diamanda.</strong> Once \"Diamanda the Magnificent, Mistress of Dragons\", a Kinet heroine. In the first game the Chaots captured her for the Synthets, whose scientists turned her into a winged Synthet warrior; her loss broke the alliance of Vitals and Kinets. Later the Vital heroine Ciara destroyed her. In Etherlords II the White Lord brings her back as a puppet. She steals the ethers of Life and Chaos, understands her master's lie, kills him, and stays to guard the closing Portal. She is also the hero of the Etherlords II training camp.</li></ul><h2>Etherlords</h2><p>The first game's heroes are soldiers of their Lords with little story of their own.</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Character</th><th>Who they are</th></tr><tr><td>The four Lords</td><td>Lord of Chaos, of Motion, of Vitality and of Synthesis: each race's ruler, who fights the White Lord in the last chapter</td></tr><tr><td>Fire Spirit</td><td>Appears near the Chaot castle for the first time in centuries; the target of the Fire Ordeal</td></tr><tr><td>Fire Konung</td><td>A mysterious being of flesh near the Wild Wasteland, whose strength comes from four Altars of War</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical Monster</td><td>A Synthet machine loose in the Vital forests</td></tr><tr><td>Keeper of the Tomb</td><td>Ghostly guardian of the ancient tomb in the Foggy Forest, with four spirit Keepers</td></tr><tr><td>Walking Horror</td><td>A Synthet monster in the Lost City</td></tr><tr><td>Chief Oracle</td><td>Synthet seer who doubted the White Lord's existence</td></tr></table><h2>Vitals of Etherlords II</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Character</th><th>Who they are</th></tr><tr><td>Eileen of Rowan clan</td><td>The heroine of the Vital campaign</td></tr><tr><td>Vian the Cypress Staff</td><td>Elder of Golden Ash-Tree City</td></tr><tr><td>Narmes</td><td>Vian's son, rescued from the Synthets</td></tr><tr><td>Tienan of Blackthorn clan</td><td>Commander of the Blackthorn Fortress</td></tr><tr><td>Kilan the Fearless</td><td>Held the White Stone outpost alone for eight days</td></tr><tr><td>Rulana the Hermit</td><td>Forest mage who foresees Diamanda's coming</td></tr><tr><td>Erna, Musician</td><td>The keeper of Golden Ash-Tree City's dragon, and the dragon who hears the Music of the Spheres</td></tr><tr><td>Deavan</td><td>Keeper of the Spring of Life, killed by Diamanda</td></tr><tr><td>Flon</td><td>A Vital traitor who serves the Synthets in hope of ruling after them</td></tr><tr><td>Ciara</td><td>Heroine of old who destroyed Diamanda</td></tr></table><h2>Chaots of Etherlords II</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Character</th><th>Who they are</th></tr><tr><td>Konnar of Fire Valley</td><td>The hero of the Chaot campaign</td></tr><tr><td>Alveig, Lightning of the West</td><td>Chief who wins the war leadership with a dragon duel and makes peace with the Kinets</td></tr><tr><td>Krogerd, Hammer of the East</td><td>Her rival, owner of the Fighting Cyclops</td></tr><tr><td>Hellvard and Sofnir</td><td>Brother chiefs; Hellvard falls to the Kinet raid</td></tr><tr><td>Sivrid</td><td>Shaman who reads the future in flames</td></tr><tr><td>Odmar Red-Fist and Norti</td><td>Brother and sister, drivers of fighting monsters</td></tr><tr><td>Great Shaman</td><td>Guardian of the Chaos Eye, killed by Diamanda</td></tr></table><h2>Kinets of Etherlords II</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Character</th><th>Who they are</th></tr><tr><td>Mirabell</td><td>The heroine of the Kinet campaign, a messenger fond of calligraphy</td></tr><tr><td>Rinalda</td><td>Noble heroine of the Council, trapped on an island by Diamanda</td></tr><tr><td>Dominicius</td><td>Head of the Council of Sixteen</td></tr><tr><td>Keeper of Keys</td><td>Plots to take the Head's seat in the White Lord's name</td></tr><tr><td>Droncan</td><td>Seer who speaks in verse</td></tr></table><h2>Synthets of Etherlords II</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Character</th><th>Who they are</th></tr><tr><td>Alpha-100F \"Steel\"</td><td>The hero of the Synthet campaign</td></tr><tr><td>Beta-600F \"Hammer\"</td><td>Hero trapped by Diamanda in a dungeon</td></tr><tr><td>Delta-600B \"Persistent\"</td><td>Coordinator of Axis City</td></tr><tr><td>Omega-300</td><td>Scientist who turned himself into a measuring device and went mad</td></tr><tr><td>Lambda-100C \"Engineer\"</td><td>Scientist who learns to summon pale creatures</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster</td><td>The Synthets' networked intelligence, which gives the orders</td></tr></table>", "search": "characters this page reveals the fates of the main characters. the named characters of both games. the two central ones have pages of their own: diamanda and the white lord. the plots are on the story of etherlords and the story of etherlords ii. the white lord and diamanda the white lord. master of white ether, who lives in the temple of time. in the first game the lord of the victorious race duels him for his throne. in etherlords ii, to escape the cycle that brings a new usurper every time of change, he opens a portal to the outer plane, raises diamanda as his tool and plans to sacrifice her. she kills him. diamanda. once \"diamanda the magnificent, mistress of dragons\", a kinet heroine. in the first game the chaots captured her for the synthets, whose scientists turned her into a winged synthet warrior; her loss broke the alliance of vitals and kinets. later the vital heroine ciara destroyed her. in etherlords ii the white lord brings her back as a puppet. she steals the ethers of life and chaos, understands her master's lie, kills him, and stays to guard the closing portal. she is also the hero of the etherlords ii training camp. etherlords the first game's heroes are soldiers of their lords with little story of their own. the four lords lord of chaos, of motion, of vitality and of synthesis: each race's ruler, who fights the white lord in the last chapter fire spirit appears near the chaot castle for the first time in centuries; the target of the fire ordeal fire konung a mysterious being of flesh near the wild wasteland, whose strength comes from four altars of war mechanical monster a synthet machine loose in the vital forests keeper of the tomb ghostly guardian of the ancient tomb in the foggy forest, with four spirit keepers walking horror a synthet monster in the lost city chief oracle synthet seer who doubted the white lord's existence vitals of etherlords ii eileen of rowan clan the heroine of the vital campaign vian the cypress staff elder of golden ash-tree city narmes vian's son, rescued from the synthets tienan of blackthorn clan commander of the blackthorn fortress kilan the fearless held the white stone outpost alone for eight days rulana the hermit forest mage who foresees diamanda's coming erna, musician the keeper of golden ash-tree city's dragon, and the dragon who hears the music of the spheres deavan keeper of the spring of life, killed by diamanda flon a vital traitor who serves the synthets in hope of ruling after them ciara heroine of old who destroyed diamanda chaots of etherlords ii konnar of fire valley the hero of the chaot campaign alveig, lightning of the west chief who wins the war leadership with a dragon duel and makes peace with the kinets krogerd, hammer of the east her rival, owner of the fighting cyclops hellvard and sofnir brother chiefs; hellvard falls to the kinet raid sivrid shaman who reads the future in flames odmar red-fist and norti brother and sister, drivers of fighting monsters great shaman guardian of the chaos eye, killed by diamanda kinets of etherlords ii mirabell the heroine of the kinet campaign, a messenger fond of calligraphy rinalda noble heroine of the council, trapped on an island by diamanda dominicius head of the council of sixteen keeper of keys plots to take the head's seat in the white lord's name droncan seer who speaks in verse synthets of etherlords ii alpha-100f \"steel\" the hero of the synthet campaign beta-600f \"hammer\" hero trapped by diamanda in a dungeon delta-600b \"persistent\" coordinator of axis city omega-300 scientist who turned himself into a measuring device and went mad lambda-100c \"engineer\" scientist who learns to summon pale creatures cluster the synthets' networked intelligence, which gives the orders"},
		{"key": "campaign-el1", "title": "Etherlords campaigns", "category": "modes", "html": "<h1>Etherlords campaigns</h1><p>Etherlords has two campaigns, each played by two of the <a href=\"wiki://races\">four races</a>:</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Campaign</th><th>Races</th></tr><tr><td>Flying Leaf</td><td>Vitals and Kinets</td></tr><tr><td>Flaming Iron</td><td>Chaots and Synthets</td></tr></table><p>Start one from <strong>New Game › Campaign</strong>. Each campaign has eight chapters. At the seventh you choose a branch, and the last chapter follows your choice. If you lose a chapter, you play it again.</p><h2>Score</h2><p>Your score for a chapter depends on how many days you needed compared with the expected number, and on the difficulty. The best scores are kept in the <strong>Hall of Fame</strong>.</p><h2>Single missions</h2><p><strong>New Game › Single Mission</strong> plays the single missions on their own, with their own saves.</p><p>See <a href=\"wiki://adventure-map\">The adventure map</a> for what happens on the map.</p><p>The plot of both campaigns is told in <a href=\"wiki://story-el1\">The story of Etherlords</a>.</p>", "search": "etherlords campaigns etherlords has two campaigns, each played by two of the four races: flying leaf vitals and kinets flaming iron chaots and synthets start one from new game › campaign. each campaign has eight chapters. at the seventh you choose a branch, and the last chapter follows your choice. if you lose a chapter, you play it again. score your score for a chapter depends on how many days you needed compared with the expected number, and on the difficulty. the best scores are kept in the hall of fame. single missions new game › single mission plays the single missions on their own, with their own saves. see the adventure map for what happens on the map. the plot of both campaigns is told in the story of etherlords."},
		{"key": "campaign-el2", "title": "Etherlords II campaigns", "category": "modes", "html": "<h1>Etherlords II campaigns</h1><p>Etherlords II has five campaigns, played in a set order. Open them from <strong>Single › Campaign</strong>.</p><ol><li>Start with the <strong>Vitals</strong> (the green Ether of Nature) or the <strong>Chaots</strong> (the red Ether of Flame).</li><li>The Vitals campaign leads on to the <strong>Synthets</strong> (the black Ether of the Machine), the Chaots campaign to the <strong>Kinets</strong> (the blue Ether of Air).</li><li>The last campaign belongs to <strong>Diamanda</strong>, the ancient creation of heroes' magic.</li></ol><p>The race campaigns have four chapters each, the last one five. The main hero, money and progress carry over between chapters. Each chapter can be played at three difficulty levels.</p><p>The remake awards 100 points per chapter won, multiplied by the difficulty level (1–3). Completing a campaign adds its total to the campaign table in <strong>Single › Mission › Hall of Fame</strong>.</p><h2>Tutorial and missions</h2><ul><li><strong>Single › Tutorial</strong> teaches the map (adventures) and battles (combat).</li><li><strong>Single › Mission</strong> plays the single missions.</li></ul><p>See <a href=\"wiki://adventure-map\">The adventure map</a> and <a href=\"wiki://races\">Races</a>.</p><p>The plot of all five campaigns is told in <a href=\"wiki://story-el2\">The story of Etherlords II</a>.</p>", "search": "etherlords ii campaigns etherlords ii has five campaigns, played in a set order. open them from single › campaign. start with the vitals (the green ether of nature) or the chaots (the red ether of flame). the vitals campaign leads on to the synthets (the black ether of the machine), the chaots campaign to the kinets (the blue ether of air). the last campaign belongs to diamanda, the ancient creation of heroes' magic. the race campaigns have four chapters each, the last one five. the main hero, money and progress carry over between chapters. each chapter can be played at three difficulty levels. the remake awards 100 points per chapter won, multiplied by the difficulty level (1–3). completing a campaign adds its total to the campaign table in single › mission › hall of fame. tutorial and missions single › tutorial teaches the map (adventures) and battles (combat). single › mission plays the single missions. see the adventure map and races. the plot of all five campaigns is told in the story of etherlords ii."},
		{"key": "duel", "title": "Duel", "category": "modes", "html": "<h1>Duel</h1><p>A duel is a single battle between two heroes, outside the campaigns. Open it from <strong>New Game › Duel</strong> (Etherlords) or <strong>Single › Duel</strong> (Etherlords II).</p><h2>Choosing heroes</h2><p>Each side picks a <strong>preset</strong>: a ready-made hero with race, level, skills, specialisation, spells, runes (Etherlords) and artifacts. The games' own presets are joined by those you make yourself. <strong>Random</strong> picks a preset of the current resource level.</p><h2>The hero editor</h2><p>The editor changes a preset. Everything costs resources, and the total must fit the chosen <strong>resource level</strong>:</p><ul><li>Etherlords: resource levels 1–5, hero levels 1–15. Each further copy of the same spell costs more.</li><li>Etherlords II: resource levels 1–3, hero levels 1–13. The bag holds 16 spells with at most 4 copies of one.</li><li>Up to five skills and five artifacts.</li></ul><p>In Etherlords II, <strong>Randomize</strong> beside the preset name builds a random hero the way the community's random deck generator does. It picks a random race, skills and an artifact, and keeps the hero level you set. It fills the deck mostly with cantrips and common spells and the reserve with rare, unique and colourless spells, mixing every colour. The preset uses resource level 6, which allows any colour without a budget. Press it again for another deck, then save it like any other preset.</p><h2>The match</h2><p>Set the number of rounds (1–99, all of them are played) and the arena, or <strong>Random</strong> for a new arena every round. A coin decides who attacks first in each round.</p><p>In Etherlords II, the remake records a duel win in the local <strong>Hall of Fame</strong> under <strong>Duels</strong>. The winner earns 100 points per round won. Open the Hall of Fame from <strong>Single › Mission</strong> and use its switch button to reach the duel table.</p><h2>HotSeat</h2><p>Two players can play on one computer (<strong>HotSeat</strong>). A message between turns tells the next player to take the seat.</p><h2>Network duel</h2><p>Open <strong>New Game › Multiplayer › Duel</strong> in Etherlords or <strong>Multiplayer</strong> in Etherlords II. Both players need the same Demiurges version and game edition. Under <strong>Hero preset</strong>, each player can pick a hero supplied with the game or a custom hero saved in the Duel editor. The game shares the preset automatically when you connect.</p><p>The host sets the arena, <strong>Resource cap</strong>, and <strong>Rounds</strong>, chooses a hero, then presses <strong>Host</strong>. Both heroes must be at or below the cap. In <strong>LAN</strong>, the waiting duels show their cap next to the arena. Select a duel and press <strong>Join</strong>, or enter the host's address and UDP port (27184 by default) in <strong>LAN</strong> or <strong>TCP/IP</strong>. After connecting, the guest sees the host's settings, picks an eligible hero, and presses <strong>Ready</strong>. For play over the internet, the UDP port must be reachable from the other player's network, or choose <strong>Online room</strong> under <strong>Connection</strong> in <strong>TCP/IP</strong>: the host gets a six-letter room code (copied to the clipboard) and the guest enters it instead of an address. No ports need to be opened, and the browser version plays online this way. If the guest's connection drops during a duel, the host waits two minutes; the guest can join again the same way and the duel continues where it stopped.</p><p>Network matches play all scheduled rounds (1–99). After each round, both players confirm the score screen to start the next battle with their chosen heroes. In Etherlords, the higher-level hero opens the first round. A coin toss decides who opens when their levels are equal and in all Etherlords II matches. The first attack alternates each round in both games. Use the desktop or Android game for network play.</p><p>Once connected, use the chat field in the lobby to talk to your opponent. In battle, press <strong>Enter</strong> or select <strong>Chat</strong> to open the conversation. Press <strong>Enter</strong> to send and <strong>Esc</strong> to close the panel. Messages stay with the current match across rounds.</p><p>In Etherlords, new messages appear at the top of the battlefield. Press <strong>~</strong> to read the match transcript in the console. In the general Options tab, clear <strong>Show chat</strong> to hide the brief battlefield messages. The transcript stays available in the console.</p><p>In Etherlords II, use <strong>New hero</strong> or <strong>Edit hero</strong> from the network lobby to open the Duel editor. Hosts can edit before hosting, and guests can edit while choosing a hero. The editor follows the current resource cap. Saving selects the new preset in the lobby.</p><p>In Etherlords II, the host can set <strong>Turn limit (s)</strong> to 10–3600 seconds, or leave it at 0 for unlimited play. Attack and defence each get a fresh clock. At zero, the active player ends the phase with their current attack or defence choices.</p><p>Between Etherlords II rounds, select <strong>Change deck</strong> on the score screen if your hero has reserve spells. Select one spell in each column and swap them. The deck stays at 16 spells, with up to four copies of each spell. The host can set a separate <strong>Deck change (s)</strong> limit of 10–3600 seconds, or 0 for unlimited. When time runs out, your staged deck is confirmed for the next round.</p><h2>Strategic multiplayer preview</h2><p>In Etherlords, open <strong>New Game › Multiplayer › Strategy</strong>, then choose <strong>Local Network</strong> or <strong>Internet</strong>. The host chooses an original map and two distinct human seats. Additional seats play as AI. On a LAN, select a waiting match under <strong>LAN games</strong> to fill in the host’s address and port. You can also enter the address and UDP port (27186 by default) directly. In <strong>Internet</strong>, <strong>Online room</strong> under <strong>Connection</strong> works the same way as for duels: the host gets a room code and the guest enters it. Both players need the same Demiurges build and original map.</p><p>The preview supports movement orders, resource sites, artifact and scroll pickups, castle assaults, and battles. During planning, both commanders can edit spellbooks, learn offered skills, and rearrange or exchange equipment between their own adjacent heroes. The hero screen can dismiss a hero with confirmation. A commander who still has a castle can continue after dismissing their last hero. Both commanders commit their orders with <strong>End Turn</strong>, then the host resolves one shared night.</p><p>When one commander fights a neutral creature, the other stays on the adventure map and sees when the battle starts and ends. The night resumes after the battle settles.</p><p>An adjacent hero can open a laboratory or component portal their commander owns. Orders to visit other shops resolve at night. The night waits for the visiting commander to finish each shop dialog. Owned shops can be upgraded through the buildings list.</p><p>Orders to enter forts also resolve at night. During planning you can upgrade a fort you own from the buildings list or release its garrison. The hero returns immediately to the tile used to enter.</p><p>If the guest leaves during a night shop visit, the host keeps the map. Captures and completed purchases stand, and any unconfirmed guest purchases are cleared. The host can finish their own open shop before the night continues.</p><p>Some maps start players with castles and no heroes. Open <strong>Global Spells</strong> and produce <strong>Summon Recruit</strong>. Once you have invested enough ether, confirm <strong>Cast</strong> to recruit a hero at your castle. Other available global spells use the same prompt, with a choice of target when needed. Cancel production to recover its material cost, or dispel one of your active spells from <strong>Global Spells</strong> or the affected hero's screen.</p><p><strong>Remote Use</strong> lets a hero trade at an explored laboratory or component portal from anywhere on the map. The hero stays put and the shop keeps its owner. The other commander can keep planning while you trade.</p><p>Open <strong>Diplomacy</strong> to propose peace terms, counter an offer, or trade resources. Use the inbox and outbox to read, decline, or withdraw offers. Resource trades settle immediately when both commanders can pay. Accepted peace terms and broken alliances take effect at the next dawn.</p><p>When hosting, set <strong>Adventure time limit</strong> in seconds or leave it at zero for unlimited planning. The clock starts when both commanders have loaded the new day. At zero it ends any uncommitted turns, dismisses open planning windows, and refunds the materials for a ready global spell while keeping the ether spent.</p><p><strong>Combat time limit</strong> sets a separate countdown for each attack and defence phase. At zero, the active player ends the phase with their current choices. Leave it at zero for untimed combat. Saved matches keep both limits and start a fresh adventure clock when both commanders reconnect.</p><p>The host can use <strong>Save</strong> or <strong>F5</strong> while both commanders are still planning. To resume, choose <strong>Load</strong> in the Strategy lobby, pick the save, and host it. The guest can find a resumed LAN match in the list or join again by address. The saved day, seats, and queued orders carry over. Both commanders must confirm <strong>End Turn</strong> again.</p><p>During a live match, <strong>Load</strong> and quick load are unavailable on the map. If the guest quits or loses the connection during planning, the host can keep playing against the AI in the same match. If the guest leaves during a battle between the two commanders, the host wins that battle and the AI takes over on the map. A result already decided stands. Save again after the AI takes over to resume that match from <strong>Load</strong> in the Strategy lobby without waiting for a guest.</p><h2>Ether Expedition</h2><p>Choose <strong>Expedition</strong> on the main menu with Etherlords II installed. Compare the starting spellbooks and hero stats before choosing a preset, then take it through three branching acts. The connections between rooms vary by run. Choose <strong>Scout next rooms</strong> to compare the opponents, life, and levels in reachable battles before choosing a room. Life carries between duels. At a battle reward, add or replace a spell or choose <strong>Recover life instead</strong>. Elite battles and the first two guardians offer extra choices, with priority given to rare and unique spells. Camps let you heal or remove a spell. From Act II, you can use a camp to replace one spell with a faction spell that removes enchantments. At the Wayside Spring, you can borrow a one-charge artifact for your next duel. It expires after that duel even if unused. The first two guardians can vary between runs. The route journal names the current guardian and gives a preparation tip. Choose <strong>Scout guardians</strong> to inspect the guardian's life, level, and starting spellbook. You can scout White Lord from the first act and draft spells for that fight. Defeating White Lord ends the run.</p><p>Expedition opponents with a placement on an original campaign map use an arena from that terrain. The room seed keeps the arena choice consistent across reloads. White Lord awaits in the citadel.</p><p>New runs give priority to opponents you have yet to face in the act. Reachable room previews match the battles you enter, and older runs keep their original matchups.</p><p>You can scout the next rooms while choosing a reward, resolving an event, or resting at camp. Select <strong>Inspect spellbook</strong> to see an opponent’s starting cards, skills, and specialization before making your current choice.</p><p>Battle rewards in new runs offer a creature, a sorcery or enchantment, and a third spell when eligible cards are available. The first two slots favor your faction. In the first two rooms, the creature slot prioritizes spells that cost at most three ether. Elite and guardian rewards still offer extra choices.</p><p>The journal header shows the run seed. To replay a route, enter that number in <strong>Run seed</strong> on the Expedition start screen and choose the same starting spellbook. Leave the field empty for a random route.</p><p>Open <strong>Journey log</strong> on the route or results screen to see which rooms you visited in each act. The log survives a checkpoint reload. Earlier acts in older checkpoints may lack room details.</p><p>The results screen lets you inspect your last opponent and review the round and remaining enemy life when recorded. <strong>Retry this seed</strong> starts a fresh run with the same starter, seed, and route rules after you confirm. Your new choices still shape the run.</p><p>Starting in Act II, you can spend a camp visit learning or improving a combat skill. Training lasts for the rest of the run. You can train twice per expedition and review the skills you earned in <strong>Deck</strong>. Each training choice shows the effect of its next rank.</p><p>Guardian scouting shows the skills and specialization used in the fight. Select the specialization to read how it works in the in-game wiki.</p><p>At the Spellwright’s Mirror, you can spend 3 life to exchange an offered common spell or cantrip for a rarer spell of the same type. Compare both spells and their ether costs before you confirm. The exchange replaces one copy and keeps your spellbook the same size.</p><p>From Act II of a new run, the Silent Reliquary can offer a keepsake for 5 life. You can carry one keepsake at a time. It starts each duel with one charge, and the <strong>Deck</strong> panel shows which one you chose.</p><p>From Act II, a new run can also encounter the <strong>Bridge Warden</strong>. Inspect the opponent's starting spellbook before accepting the duel. A win earns an elite reward, a loss ends the expedition, and walking away lets you continue the route.</p><p>Open <strong>Deck</strong> to check spell counts, creature stats, and the average base ether cost. When replacing a spell, select the outgoing card to compare it with the reward before confirming.</p><p>Press <strong>Esc</strong> to step back from the deck or spell comparison. From the list of spells to replace, <strong>Esc</strong> returns to the reward offers.</p><p>The game saves when you enter a room, make a choice, or finish a combat action. If saving a battle result fails, choose <strong>Retry save</strong> to keep the result before continuing. <strong>Resume expedition</strong> restores the current room, including the battlefield, hands, and phase of an unfinished duel. Older room-only saves resume from the duel's opening.</p><p>If you already have a saved run, <strong>Begin expedition</strong> asks before replacing it. Choose <strong>Keep saved expedition</strong> to return to the starter and seed you entered.</p><h2>Replays</h2><p>Etherlords II duels can be saved and watched again (<strong>Replay</strong> on the Duel screen). After a completed local or network match, choose <strong>Save duel replay</strong> on the final score screen. The game writes one replay per round and includes each round's starting decks and both players' combat actions. Network replays open from the recording player's side. The engine reads the original replays and writes its own.</p><p>See <a href=\"wiki://combat\">How a battle works</a>.</p>", "search": "duel a duel is a single battle between two heroes, outside the campaigns. open it from new game › duel (etherlords) or single › duel (etherlords ii). choosing heroes each side picks a preset: a ready-made hero with race, level, skills, specialisation, spells, runes (etherlords) and artifacts. the games' own presets are joined by those you make yourself. random picks a preset of the current resource level. the hero editor the editor changes a preset. everything costs resources, and the total must fit the chosen resource level: etherlords: resource levels 1–5, hero levels 1–15. each further copy of the same spell costs more. etherlords ii: resource levels 1–3, hero levels 1–13. the bag holds 16 spells with at most 4 copies of one. up to five skills and five artifacts. in etherlords ii, randomize beside the preset name builds a random hero the way the community's random deck generator does. it picks a random race, skills and an artifact, and keeps the hero level you set. it fills the deck mostly with cantrips and common spells and the reserve with rare, unique and colourless spells, mixing every colour. the preset uses resource level 6, which allows any colour without a budget. press it again for another deck, then save it like any other preset. the match set the number of rounds (1–99, all of them are played) and the arena, or random for a new arena every round. a coin decides who attacks first in each round. in etherlords ii, the remake records a duel win in the local hall of fame under duels. the winner earns 100 points per round won. open the hall of fame from single › mission and use its switch button to reach the duel table. hotseat two players can play on one computer (hotseat). a message between turns tells the next player to take the seat. network duel open new game › multiplayer › duel in etherlords or multiplayer in etherlords ii. both players need the same demiurges version and game edition. under hero preset, each player can pick a hero supplied with the game or a custom hero saved in the duel editor. the game shares the preset automatically when you connect. the host sets the arena, resource cap, and rounds, chooses a hero, then presses host. both heroes must be at or below the cap. in lan, the waiting duels show